        "full_content": "Canon's Pixma TS6520 wireless all-in-one inkjet is a true back-to-school printer pick for $79\nThe impressive feature-set includes print, scan, copy, and automatic duplex printing with a 1.42-inch OLED display\nCanon's Pixma line has been the default recommendation for home printing for years, and the TS6520 is the compact, back-to-school-ready all-in-one built for exactly that kind of everyday use.\nRight now, the Canon Pixma TS6520 has dropped to $79 (was $150) at Amazon. This wireless color inkjet handles printing, scanning, and copying, with automatic two-sided printing to save on paper and a 1.42-inch OLED display for checking ink levels and adjusting settings without needing a phone app.\nPrint, scan, and copy functionality with automatic duplex (2-sided) printing, wireless connectivity through the Canon PRINT app, Apple AirPrint, and Mopria Print Service, and a 1.42-inch OLED display for monitoring ink levels and settings. Compact design intended to fit easily on a desk or shelf.\nWhy we recommend it\nCanon's PIXMA series has built its reputation on being the printer most people default to for home use, and the TS6520 keeps that formula simple: reliable printing and scanning, automatic duplexing that saves paper without extra steps, and setup that requires little more than connecting to Wi-Fi. For students heading back to school or anyone who just wants a printer that works without fuss, that simplicity is the whole appeal.\nThe compact design and OLED display are nice touches at this price. Many budget all-in-ones skip a proper display entirely, and being able to check ink levels and adjust settings without opening an app on your phone is a small but genuinely convenient feature.\nFor more top performing units, see our guides to the best home printers, best inkjet printers, and best ink tank printers we've tested.\nAlso consider\nCombines color printing with a flatbed scanner and copier and supports Wi-Fi and USB connectivity. Print speeds reach 11 pages per minute in black and six pages per minute in color, while maximum color resolution is 4800 x 1200 dpi.\nRefillable EcoTank ink system with up to 2 years of ink included in the box, automatic document feeder (ADF) for multi-page scans and copies, fax capability, and both wireless and Ethernet connectivity. Print, scan, and copy handled through the Epson Smart Panel app.\nRefillable ink tank system with 2 years of ink included in the box, print, scan, and copy functionality, and wireless connectivity over 2.4GHz Wi-Fi. Mobile printing supported through the HP Smart app.\nPrice Context & Historical Value\nA $70.99 discount off $149.99 works out to a substantial 47 percent off, one of the larger cuts we've seen on a Canon Pixma model. Over Black Friday, it dropped to an all-time low of $64, but the general price for most of the year fluctuates between $100 and $140. So, this current discount makes this a genuinely strong back-to-school price point for an inkjet printer.\nIf you want to save on other units, see our round-up of this week's best printer deals.\nShould you buy it?\n✅ Buy the Canon PIXMA TS6520 if...\nYou need a reliable, no-frills printer for school assignments and home use, you print double-sided documents often and want automatic duplexing, or you want a compact printer that doesn't take over your desk.\n❌ Skip the Canon PIXMA TS6520 if...\nYou need an automatic document feeder for scanning multi-page documents, you print high volumes regularly and want a refillable ink tank system, or you're looking for a printer we've tested hands-on.\nThe Catch: What to know before you buy\nWe haven't reviewed this specific printer hands-on, so this recommendation leans on Canon's general reputation with the Pixma line rather than direct testing.\nLike most inkjet printers, ongoing cartridge costs are worth factoring in if you print often. It also lacks an automatic document feeder (ADF), so scanning or copying multi-page documents means feeding them in one at a time.\nNo ADF is unlikely to be an issue for most home and student printing - we expect that feature on printers for office work and business, but it's worth mentioning.\nSign up to the TechRadar Pro newsletter to get all the top news, opinion, features and guidance your business needs to succeed!\nBryan M. Wolfe is a staff writer at TechRadar, iMore, and wherever Future can use him. Though his passion is Apple-based products, he doesn't have a problem using Windows and Android. Bryan's a single father of a 15-year-old daughter and a puppy, Isabelle. Thanks for reading!\nYou must confirm your public display name before commenting\nPlease logout and then login again, you will then be prompted to enter your display name.",
