        "title": "12 Things in Your Home That Can Become More Dangerous as You Get Older",
        "link": "https://www.savingadvice.com/articles/2026/08/21/10744987_12-things-in-your-home-that-can-become-more-dangerous-as-you-get-older.html",
        "source": "SavingAdvice.com Blog",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T22:18:41.142422+00:00",
        "image": "https://etyb2wffn3d.exactdn.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/shutterstock_1012799926.jpg?strip=all",
        "full_content": "The house you’ve lived in safely for 20 or 30 years doesn’t suddenly become dangerous on your 65th birthday, but the way you interact with it can change. Vision, hearing, reflexes, muscle strength, balance, and mobility can shift with age, turning ordinary household features into hazards that were easy to ignore when you were younger. The National Institute on Aging reports that more than one in four adults age 65 and older falls each year, while CDC data show about 300,000 hip-fracture hospitalizations related to older-adult falls annually. The encouraging part is that many home hazards for older adults can be reduced with inexpensive changes rather than a major renovation. These 12 familiar things deserve a second look as you (or someone you love) gets older.\n1. Throw Rugs Can Become Trip Traps\nThat decorative rug beside the bed or kitchen sink may have been there for years without causing a problem, but it can become surprisingly hazardous when balance or gait changes. The National Institute on Aging recommends avoiding throw rugs and small area rugs and making sure carpeting is firmly secured to the floor. Rug corners can curl, backing can slide, and even a small change in floor height can catch a shoe, slipper, cane, or walker. If you want to keep a rug, use appropriate non-slip backing or secure it properly rather than assuming its weight will keep it in place. Removing unnecessary rugs is one of the simplest ways to eliminate home hazards for older adults without spending much money.\n2. Bathtubs and Showers Become Higher-Risk Areas\nBathrooms combine several things that increase fall risk: hard surfaces, water, tight spaces, and movements that require balance. The CDC recommends installing grab bars inside and outside tubs and showers and beside toilets as part of older-adult fall prevention. Nonslip surfaces can also help, while relying on a towel rack for support is risky because it may not be designed to bear someone’s weight. Think about the entire sequence of getting into the shower, bathing, stepping out wet, reaching for a towel, and getting dressed rather than evaluating only the tub itself. Bathroom safety becomes especially important among home hazards for older adults because one slip can have much greater consequences later in life.\n3. Stairs Need More Attention Than They Used To\nA staircase you’ve climbed thousands of times can become more challenging when depth perception, vision, strength, or balance changes. The NIA recommends secure handrails on both sides of stairs, good lighting, and keeping steps free of shoes, papers, clothing, and other clutter. Carrying laundry downstairs can be particularly risky if the basket blocks your view or prevents you from gripping the railing. Loose carpet, uneven steps, and missing handrails deserve repairs rather than becoming inconveniences everyone learns to work around. The goal isn’t necessarily avoiding stairs entirely but making every trip up and down less dependent on perfect balance.\n4. Poor Lighting Can Turn Ordinary Rooms Into Hazards\nA dim hallway that once felt cozy can become a problem when it makes furniture, cords, steps, or changes in flooring harder to see. The CDC recommends brighter home lighting for older adults, while the NIA suggests switches at both ends of long hallways and motion-activated lights around pathways and stairwells. Pay particular attention to the route between the bedroom and bathroom because that trip may happen when you’re sleepy and the house is dark. Nightlights and motion sensors can reduce the temptation to walk several feet through darkness searching for a switch. Better illumination is one of those home hazards for older adults fixes that can improve convenience at the same time it improves safety.\n5. Extension Cords Can Become Invisible Obstacles\nExtension cords and charging cables have multiplied in many homes as phones, tablets, lamps, televisions, and other devices compete for outlets. The NIA recommends keeping electrical cords close to walls and away from normal walking paths. A cable stretched between a recliner and an outlet may barely register visually, especially in dim lighting or when the cord closely matches the floor. Avoid routing cords beneath rugs or creating makeshift electrical arrangements that can introduce additional fire hazards. Walk through the home specifically looking downward for cables, because familiar clutter can become almost invisible when you see it every day.\n6. Step Stools Can Turn a Small Task Into a Serious Fall\nReaching the top kitchen cabinet may have once involved casually climbing onto a chair, but that habit becomes increasingly risky when balance and strength decline. The Consumer Product Safety Commission recommends addressing fall hazards throughout the home, while its older-consumer safety guidance recommends stable step stools with a top handrail rather than unsafe climbing. A better solution is often moving frequently used dishes, food, cleaning supplies, and clothing to shelves that can be reached without climbing at all. The NIA also suggests using a reach stick or asking someone for help rather than standing on a chair or table. R\nOne useful test is to identify everything you reach for at least once a week. If any of those items require climbing, stretching overhead, or standing on tiptoe, move them between roughly waist and shoulder height. The safest step stool may ultimately be the one you no longer need to use.\n7. Low Furniture Can Get in Your Way\nCoffee tables, ottomans, footstools, and decorative furniture can narrow the walking path through a room. NIA guidance recommends arranging furniture, particularly low coffee tables, so objects aren’t in your way as you walk. Furniture height matters too because very low sofas and chairs can become difficult to get out of safely when leg strength or mobility changes. Look at the route between the places you use most—the couch, kitchen, bathroom, bedroom, and front door—and make those pathways wide and predictable. A room can still be comfortable and attractive without forcing someone to weave around furniture every time they cross it.\n8. Hot Water Can Cause Burns Faster Than You Expect\nScalding is another household risk that can be overlooked when evaluating an aging-in-place home. NIA safety guidance recommends setting the water heater to 120 degrees Fahrenheit to help prevent burns from overly hot tap water. Someone with reduced sensation or slower reactions may not pull away from dangerously hot water as quickly as they once did. Check the actual water temperature rather than assuming an old heater’s thermostat is perfectly calibrated. Water temperature deserves a place beside falls when discussing home hazards for older adults because bathrooms and kitchens present both burn and slip risks.\n9. Space Heaters and Fireplaces Require Extra Caution\nPortable heaters can make one room comfortable without heating the whole house, but they introduce a fire hazard that deserves respect. The CPSC identifies fires as another leading cause of death among older adults and advises careful use and maintenance of space heaters, fireplaces, and fuel-burning appliances. Keep combustible materials away from heating equipment and replace damaged or questionable equipment rather than continuing to use it because “it still works.” Smoke alarms and carbon monoxide alarms also need to be installed appropriately and kept operational.\nThis is also a good time to review alarms rather than simply checking whether one exists somewhere in the house. CPSC recommends smoke alarms inside every bedroom, outside each sleeping area, and on every level of the home, along with carbon monoxide alarms outside sleeping areas. Test existing alarms and replace equipment according to the manufacturer’s instructions rather than assuming an alarm is working because it is still attached to the ceiling. A warmer room isn’t worth creating a fire or carbon monoxide risk.\n10. Kitchen Cooking Can Become Riskier\nCooking involves heat, sharp tools, heavy pans, spills, reaching, and sometimes open flames, making the kitchen another room worth reassessing. The NIA recommends keeping frequently used cookware and utensils within easy reach, cleaning spills immediately, and even preparing food while seated when fatigue or balance is a concern. The CPSC has also identified cooking fires and clothing ignition while cooking as important hazards for older consumers. Consider whether heavy cookware, high shelves, loose sleeves, or an awkwardly positioned microwave now make everyday meal preparation harder than necessary. Small changes in storage and routine can reduce home hazards for older adults without taking away someone’s ability to cook independently.\n11. Pets Can Become an Unexpected Fall Hazard\nA beloved dog or cat isn’t dangerous simply because its owner gets older, but an animal underfoot can create a genuine tripping risk. The NIA specifically advises older adults to know where pets are whenever they are standing or walking so an animal doesn’t cause a fall. Feeding bowls, toys, leashes, and pet beds can also wind up directly in walking paths. Create designated places for pet supplies and consider lighting that makes a dark-colored pet easier to see during nighttime trips through the house. Keeping a pet and making the home safer are entirely compatible—you just need to include the animal in your fall-prevention plan.\n12. Bed Rails Aren’t Automatically a Safety Upgrade\nBed rails can look like an obvious solution for someone worried about falling out of bed, but this is one safety purchase that deserves careful research. The Consumer Product Safety Commission says 90% of injuries associated with adult portable bed rails involve entrapment of the head, chest, or neck, and more than 3 million rails have been covered by recalls and safety warnings since 2021.\nOne reason these hazards are easy to overlook is familiarity. You stop noticing the extension cord you’ve stepped over for six years, the loose rug beside the bed, or the coffee table you’ve always walked around. A useful home-safety inspection therefore requires deliberately looking at familiar rooms as though you’re seeing them for the first time.\nDangerous gaps can form between a rail and mattress or surrounding furniture, creating entrapment and asphyxiation hazards. Before using one, check whether the model has been recalled, verify that it complies with current safety requirements, and follow the manufacturer’s installation instructions exactly. Families should also consider whether a bed rail is actually appropriate for the person’s mobility and cognitive needs rather than assuming adding one automatically makes the bedroom safer.\nYour Home Should Change as Your Needs Change\nA safer home does not have to look like a hospital or require a five-figure remodeling project. The National Institute on Aging recommends reevaluating home safety periodically because people’s needs can change over time, beginning with obvious hazards such as poor lighting, loose railings, clutter, and unsafe walking surfaces. One reason these dangers are easy to miss is familiarity: after stepping around the same extension cord or coffee table for years, you may barely notice it anymore. Walk through your home tonight and look specifically for anything that requires exceptional balance, climbing, reaching, bending, night vision, or quick reactions, then fix the easiest hazards first.\nWhich ordinary thing in your home do you think could become harder or more dangerous to use as you get older? Share your thoughts in the comments.\nWhat to Read Next\nHome Safety Checks That Can Help Older Adults Stay Independent Longer\n7 Safety Habits Every Senior Living Alone Should Master\nDrew Blankenship is a seasoned personal finance and lifestyle writer with more than a decade of professional writing experience crafting clear, actionable advice that helps savers and investors over 40 protect their wealth and make smarter everyday decisions. His bylines appear regularly on SavingAdvice.com, CleverDude.com, and other respected outlets, where he draws on deep industry knowledge to deliver practical insights on cost control, smart spending, and long-term financial security.",
        "content": "The house you’ve lived in safely for 20 or 30 years doesn’t suddenly become dangerous on your 65th birthday, but the way you interact with it can change. Vision, hearing, reflexes, muscle strength, balance, and mobility can shift with age, turning ordinary household features into hazards that were easy to ignore when you were younger. The National Institute on Aging reports that more than one in f...",
