        "title": "Play fair- Defence tells State as Moagi trial stalls",
        "link": "https://www.mmegi.bw/news/play-fair-defence-tells-state-as-moagi-trial-stalls/news",
        "source": "https://www.mmegi.bw",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T22:20:57.840529+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.mmegi.bw/mmegi/uploads/images/2026/06/23/174252.jpg",
        "full_content": "Few days after former minister Lefoko Moagi raised concerns over the late furnishing of statements, the issue resurfaced again today at the start of his trial, with defence urging prosecution to ‘play fair’ forcing adjournment of trial.\nMoagi appeared before Broadhurst Regional Magistrate today for commencement of his second trial involving corruption and money laundering charges. It is alleged that he used his position as the minister to obtain valuable consideration for Seiyuan Electric Botswana (Pty) Ltd and in return he got 100 heifers and one bull valued at P417 500. On the money laundering charge, it is alleged that he falsified a loan contract with a representative of the aforementioned company for an amount of P417 500 which was allegedly used to purchase cattle at the centre of his charges.\nAs the two sides signaled that they are ready to proceed with commencement of trial, a State witness Han Li was called to the dock, to take an oath and give his testimony. Shortly after the witness introduced himself to the court, defence counsel Tengo Rubadiri quickly objected to the testimony of Li, arguing that the accused is being ambushed. “We honestly do not have a statement of this witness. Let us start well to this trial,” Rubadiri argued.\nRubadiri argued that Moagi will suffer prejudice at the hands of the State as he has not acclimatised himself with the statement. He stressed that what the State is doing is not right. “You bring a statement five minutes before the start of a trial that is not courtesy. The State’s courtesy should be here we are let us give you an opportunity to go over the statement to be fair,” he said. In an attempt to play on a level ground, the defence sought an adjournment of the trial.\n“In order to be fair to the accused I am going to seek an adjournment. Until such time my client (Moagi) has an opportunity to see what kind of a case he has as it has been denied to him,”he said. “By virtue of the statement that should have been given to him in good time before the trial starts. This is purely to be fair. We seek an adjournment,” defence said. To put an end to the back and forth over late issuance of statements, defence pleaded that they be furnished with all original statements today.\nState prosecutor Modibedi Tladi acceded to the submission made by defence. “We subscribe to the notion and we don’t oppose the adjournment,” Tladi said. After listening to the two sides, Regional Magistrate Mareledi Dipate agreed that the defence should not be ambushed. “The State should give the statements before the end of today so that we don’t ambush them. The trial will continue tomorrow,” Dipate ordered.",
