        "full_content": "The Miss USA and Miss Teen USA Pageants Got Yanked Off the CW Just One Week Before They Were Set to Air\nMiss USA and Miss Teen USA won’t air on The CW this year, though the network paid for the rights just two years ago. The pageants will be sent straight to streaming.\nMiss Teen USA was set for Wednesday, Aug. 26, and Miss USA for Thursday, Aug. 27. Both will now stream on Queen Beauty Network, the same site that carried the events in 2025 when The CW skipped them amid chaotic leadership within the pageant organization.\nThe last time The CW aired the pageants, in 2024, Miss USA was a ratings hit. The network called it their number one new special of the year, with 1.1 million viewers. Speculation is that the continuing leadership drama is what’s standing in the way of airtime.\nThe CW’s statement kept it vague: “After careful consideration and conversations with the Miss USA Organization, The CW Network has decided to forego next week’s broadcasts of the Miss USA and Miss Teen USA pageants.” The organization pointed to terms it couldn’t settle, saying it “identified a contractual issue with our production arrangements that could not be resolved within the necessary time frame.”\nThe CW signed a three-year deal in 2024. The broadcast partnership was struck under former CEO Laylah Rose, who was later replaced after the Miss Universe Organization handed a ten-year license to Brodeur Beauty founder Thom Brodeur.\nThe turmoil hasn’t stopped there. Under Rose, Miss USA 2023 Noelia Voigt and Miss Teen USA UmaSofia Srivastava both gave up their titles, with Voigt accusing Rose of a toxic workplace. This year, the organization stripped Miss North Carolina USA Brittany Boltinhouse of her title over her conduct.\nBoltinhouse alleges her title was stripped due to her conservative Christian beliefs. She is a member of The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints. However, Boltinhouse was accused of making racist, homophobic, and transphobic comments in old social media posts that resurfaced after she won the Miss North Carolina 2026 title. The recirculated tweets include the n-word, for which Boltinhouse has said she’s “not sorry”.\nEnjoyed this story? Give us a follow on Facebook, and find plenty more on the Thought Catalog website.",
