    {
        "title": "Music piece unveiled in Tehran marking 40th day since burial of martyred Leader",
        "link": "https://www.tehrantimes.com/news/529260/Music-piece-unveiled-in-Tehran-marking-40th-day-since-burial",
        "source": "",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T21:38:57.180821+00:00",
        "image": "https://media.tehrantimes.com/d/t/2026/08/21/4/6141020.jpg?ts=1787315538202",
        "full_content": "Music piece unveiled in Tehran marking 40th day since burial of martyred Leader\nTEHRAN- Iranian vocalist and composer Hesameddin Seraj has unveiled his new music piece “Brokenhearted Spring” at a memorial ceremony marking the 40th day since the burial of Iran’s martyred leader.\nThe piece was unveiled on Thursday, at the Art Garden Pavilion (Kushk Art Garden) in Tehran as part of a special program, held to commemorate the 40th day since the burial of Ayatollah Seyyed Ali Hosseini Khamenei.\nThe ceremony was organized by Tehran Music House in cooperation with the Abbasabad Cultural and Tourism Zone.\n“Brokenhearted Spring” features lyrics by poet and lyricist Abdoljabbar Kakai, with Seraj composing and performing the piece. Davoud Varzideh was responsible for its arrangement and musical supervision.\nThe composition brings together Iranian traditional instruments and Western orchestral elements. A string orchestra featuring violin, viola and cello is combined with traditional Iranian instruments including the ney, tar, oud and santur, along with percussion and choir.\nThe musical work was presented as part of a broader cultural and artistic program focusing on the relationship between art and social responsibility and featuring works produced in different artistic fields, including poetry, music, song, recitation, graphic art and animation.\n“Brokenhearted Spring” creates a reflective and mournful atmosphere, drawing on themes of loss and remembrance through a combination of traditional Iranian musical elements and orchestral arrangements.\nAyatollah Seyyed Ali Hosseini Khamenei, the Leader of the Islamic Revolution, was martyred on February 28, 2026, in a US-Israeli strike on Tehran. His martyrdom came at the outset of a military aggression against Iran that also claimed the lives of a number of senior Iranian military commanders, officials and members of his family.\nFollowing his martyrdom, Iran entered a period of national mourning, with officials announcing a series of farewell and funeral ceremonies across the country. The ceremonies were eventually held over several days in Tehran, the holy city of Qom and Mashhad, while separate commemorative processions were also held in Iraq, including in the holy cities of Najaf and Karbala. The funeral ceremonies brought together millions of mourners who gathered to pay their final respects to the late Leader.\nThe first major farewell ceremonies were held in Tehran, where mourners gathered at the Imam Khomeini Mosalla on July 4 and 5. A funeral procession was subsequently held in the capital on July 6, followed by another ceremony in Qom on July 7. At the request of Iraqi religious and cultural figures, farewell ceremonies were also held in Najaf and Karbala on July 8, before the late Leader’s body was transferred back to Iran for the final stage of the funeral rites.\nOn July 9, the final funeral ceremony was held in Mashhad, the birthplace of Ayatollah Khamenei. His body was taken through the city amid large crowds of mourners before being laid to rest at the holy shrine of Imam Reza (AS), one of the most revered religious sites in Iran and the country’s most important pilgrimage destination. The burial marked the conclusion of a week of nationwide mourning and farewell ceremonies.\nThe funeral ceremonies were attended by large numbers of Iranian mourners as well as foreign guests and delegations. The scale of the gatherings reflected the significance of the event for supporters of the Islamic Republic, while the ceremonies also provided an opportunity for cultural and artistic communities to express their grief through poetry, music, visual arts and other forms of artistic expression.\nSAB/",
