        "full_content": "Last month, I put out a call for listeners to share the most random ways they’ve made extra money, and the responses did not disappoint!\nSome side hustles start with a business plan. Others start with a stranger in a parking garage asking if you want to get paid to drink beer.\nThat one actually happened to me in Atlanta. The woman worked for a market research company, and they needed taste-testers for a product that hadn’t launched yet.\nI invited a couple of friends along, and we each made about $75 sampling what turned out to be Miller Chill.\nLater we lived near a place called the National Food Lab, so I went over and did a few more paid taste-testings.\nTune into Episode 751 of the Side Hustle Show to learn:\n- the strangest things listeners have been paid to do\n- how a few of those random gigs turned into real businesses\n- where to find unusual rental and marketplace opportunities\nSponsors\n- Quo (formerly OpenPhone) — Try it for free!\n- Shopify — Sign up for a $1 per month trial!\n- Gusto — Get 3 months free of the leading payroll, benefits, and HR provider for modern small businesses!\n- Wealthfront — Start earning up to 4.30% variable APY today! Terms and conditions apply.\n- Monarch — Get an extended 30-day free trial!\n1. Loading Chicken Trucks\nJoe Saul-Sehy from Stacking Benjamins kicked things off with a story from his teenage years. He got hired to go out to a farm and help load chickens onto a truck.\nThe job sounded simple. Reach into the coop, grab a chicken, put it in the travel coop, but real life was different.\nThe birds panic and escape, and you chase them around a farm apologizing.\nThe “right” technique (grab both legs, pull hard, wing damage optional) was enough for Joe and his brother to quit at the first break.\nAs Joe put it, “that is the weirdest and the worst side hustle I ever had.”\nMy own one-day teenage gig was cutting out organic dog biscuits in a commercial kitchen for about $10 an hour. Tedious, and it didn’t smell great. But no chickens were involved, so I’ll take it.\n2. Uber Eats Delivery, But on a Bike\nCody Berman, author of Retire by 30 and host of The Financial Independence Show, moved to Australia for six months while his wife studied abroad.\nRejected from 15+ jobs in Australia, Cody signed up for Uber Eats — but with no license, he switched to bike delivery, and with no bike, he bought a too-small, single-gear pink bike for $25 on Gumtree, in a hilly suburb.\nHe earned a few one-star reviews for sweating on people’s food.\nHis tips:\n- Get a bike that actually works\n- Go electric if you’re somewhere hilly\n- Or just pick a different hustle\n3. Selling Snacks at School Lunch\nChandler Bolt from Self-Publishing.com got his start at Scout Camp. His mom sent him with a bunch of snacks, and he came home with a wad of cash and a switchblade knife.\nIn high school, he turned that into a business for his entrepreneurship class. Every two weeks, he and a friend loaded up a Prius at Sam’s Club until the wheels almost scrubbed, then worked the lunches selling drinks and snacks.\nThey made $8,000 in profit in about two and a half months. Chandler kept $2,000. As he said, he got paid to go to school. It worked so well that the school shut it down the next year.\nI did the same thing at Scout camp, loading up at Costco and undercutting the camp trading post. And our youngest just sold $3 shave ice with a friend on a hot day — about $25 each for an hour’s work.\n4. Selling Empty Smartphone Boxes\nJason Butler runs My Money Chronicles, a site about personal finance and side hustles. He’s flipped plenty of normal things: clothes, toys, board games.\nThe surprise was empty phone boxes. He’d read that someone online was selling them, so he listed a couple of Samsung boxes. They sold within a week.\nThere’s a whole economy in “worthless” items apparently. An original box can double or triple a resale price (used iPhones, Jordans, LEGO sets), so resellers pay $20-$25 for an empty one.\nSame with vintage manuals. (A Charizard-shaped Cheeto once sold for $350, then resold for $87,000 — so check the snack drawer.)\nRelated: The Best Items to Flip for a Profit.\n5. Buying Out a Yarn Shop\nDonna Westerkamp walked into a yarn shop that was going out of business.\nShe asked what they’d do with leftover inventory, got a call from the owner, lowballed a price — and suddenly owned the whole stock.\nShe resold it on eBay and Craigslist, roughly recouping her cost. “The inventory was atrocious… but I made a ton of money,” she said.\nDonna was already in the crafting niche, which matters.\nThat’s what Shannon Jean recommended in Episode 731: start with what you know.\n6. A $5,000 Yard Sale (and a Free Facebook Strategy)\nBill Fortenberry built his company around a strategy he created for his dad’s business, which had a Facebook page and no marketing budget.\nHe calls it the 1-10-10 strategy:\n- 1 post a week\n- 10+ photos per post\n- shared in 10+ local Facebook groups\nBill needed test cases. Around the same time, he and his wife were downsizing and raising money for a missions trip to Ukraine to help at a youth camp for kids affected by the war. So they held a yard sale.\nHe took 30 or 40 pictures, put them in a single Facebook post, explained what the money was for, and shared it in local community groups. That one post brought in more than $5,000.\nJeff Rose covered another angle in Episode 706 — the Facebook content monetization program, earning $1,000 a day.\n7. E-Bike Rentals\nWanting something to run around a full-time job, Brian Sadler from Wisconsin delivers e-bike rentals to nearby trails, campgrounds, and resort areas.\nHe did it the smart way:\n- built the website first\n- borrowed a neighbor’s bike to test the concept\n- and befriended campground owners who loved having a new guest amenity (some via paid partnerships).\nThe lesson: go where your customers already are. We have a scooter episode with Lenny Tim, who spent a couple of years on local SEO before buying a single scooter.\n8. Renting Out Your Backyard to Dogs\nMarie Frey from Nova Scotia has three kids four and under, including twins, so passive income was the goal.\nShe’s also a real estate investor, and author of The First 3 Doors: Lessons From Our Rookie Years in Real Estate Investing.\nShe found Sniffspot, which works like Airbnb for dogs. Dog owners book her backyard by the hour. Setup took a couple of pictures and a price. Bookings are accepted automatically, so there’s no management, and she can block off times when the family wants the yard.\nThey made about $500 the first year and a little less the second. For something this passive, Marie says any extra money is a win. There’s no labor either — no dog walking, no boarding.\nSimilar marketplaces worth a look:\n- Swimply — rent your pool (hosts earn $5k+/yr; insurance included)\n- Neighbor — rent storage space (garage, shed, side yard, attic). Our Neighbor review.\n9. Board Game Rentals\nTrish is in Kitsap County, Washington, near Seattle. She rents out board games, in her words, “like they’re DVDs from 2004.”\nShe runs it through her local Board Game Geek guild and rents through the Libib app. She’s building a subscription where people pay $20 a month to borrow four games.\nShe’s up to over 300 games, all labeled, scanned, and organized like a tiny Blockbuster.\nIt started because she loves board games as a hobby but can’t read rule books at night, so she’s building a business around daylight gaming.\nI like the subscription model better than per-rental. There are challenges to solve — shipping, missing pieces — but it’s a great niche.\n10. Renting Out Baby Gear\nAriana Johnson became El Salvador’s first BabyQuip provider — renting car seats, cribs, and strollers to visiting families.\nChatting with them during deliveries, she spotted a gap and launched the blog El Salvador Kids Guide, which now brings sponsored reels and paid trip-planning consultations.\nThe two hustles feed each other. Some families find the guide and then rent gear; others rent first and come back for advice.\nIf you’re already the go-to travel planner in your circle, that’s a skill you can monetize — see Episode 670 with Bailey Richert.\n11. Digital Nomad Co-Living and Retreats\nDanny Flood had been traveling for years as a digital nomad, selling courses online through a partnership with AppSumo.\nA friend in Greece invited him to a villa in the countryside, and Danny started organizing co-living retreats there.\nThe first one had three people, four including him. The next year he expanded to Nepal. Two years later, roughly 35 or 36 people came to the co-living in Pokhara.\nThen people suggested a summit. He hosted one with more than 120 participants, with the Nepali government and tourism board attending. He’s back in September for the co-living and a second summit at digitalnomadsnepal.com, with about 40 speakers.\nHis advice: find a location that isn’t saturated, run it in shoulder season, pick a clear niche, and use good photos. Some of his first posts were just on Reddit.\nIf events appeal to you, try Episode 634 with Chloe Winstanley, or Episode 297 with Hung Pham on The Culture Summit.\n12. Online English Lessons\nMonica is the founder of English with Monica. She went from 524 TikTok followers to 70,000 in a year by going live four times a week and actually engaging.\nThat audience unlocked bootcamps, digital products, and memberships.\nThere are dozens of sites where you can teach English online, but it’s usually one-on-one, and typical rates might be $10-25 an hour.\nIf you build your own audience like Monica did, you become a market of one — and that changes what you can earn from the same skill.\n13. Turning “Can You Help With My Resumé?” Into a Business\nTwo callers, nearly the same story — both started by request, not marketing:\nCyril (DareToCloud.com)\nCyril, a guitar teacher, was asked by a student’s mom to help with her CV.\nHe was great at it, got her up to speed on a required CRM, and she landed her dream job.\nHis realization: the real skill was communicating clearly to a specific person — “a CV is basically a landing page for a human.”\nThat’s now what he does for a living (website maintenance and SEO for B2B companies).\nBalkisu Fidelis (Kisu’s CVs Collection)\nBalkisu started by helping a friend apply for a job. Later that month, the friend referred someone else. Before long, people were paying her to write and improve CVs and cover letters.\nBoth started by request, not from marketing. That’s a great sign — if people ask for your help without you even trying, that’s real perceived expertise.\nYears ago, Austin Church from Episode 417 told us to pay attention to what wants to happen. There’s also the Tim Ferriss line: what would this look like if it were easy?\nIf a project has sat on the back burner for years, maybe it doesn’t want to happen. And that’s okay.\n14. Affiliate Marketing Without an Audience\nAnthony Smith makes money recommending digital marketing products — no big audience, funnels, or paid ads. Here’s an example of his Stan Store.\nHis first sales came from simply sharing useful info with people already looking to grow their businesses.\nHis takeaways:\n- People respond to authenticity — you don’t have to be an expert\n- Consistency beats perfection\nThat’s sales 101 — be a listener first and a problem solver second.\nYour Turn\nBig thanks to everyone who submitted a clip. I’m sorry I wasn’t able to air all of them!\nIf you have a fun, random, or creative side hustle to share, I’d love to hear it and possibly feature you in an upcoming episode.\nEpisode Links\nLooking for More Side Hustle Help?\n- Start Your Free $500 Challenge. My free 5-day email course shows you how to add $500 to your bottom line.\n- Join the free Side Hustle Nation Community. The free Facebook group is the best place to connect with other side hustlers and get your questions answered.\n- Download The Side Hustle Show. My free podcast shares how to make extra money with actionable weekly episodes.",
