    {
        "title": "Why global visitors are shopping in Shenzhen's Huaqiangbei",
        "link": "https://news.cgtn.com/news/2026-08-15/Why-global-visitors-are-shopping-in-Shenzhen-s-Huaqiangbei-1PCyrXwvENq/p.html?UTM_Source=cgtn&UTM_Medium=rss&UTM_Campaign=China",
        "source": "China",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T21:38:57.177844+00:00",
        "image": "https://news.cgtn.com/news/2026-08-15/Why-global-visitors-are-shopping-in-Shenzhen-s-Huaqiangbei-1PCyrXwvENq/img/e8b1f345d63f406e8c21ac84bed52322/e8b1f345d63f406e8c21ac84bed52322-1280.jpeg",
        "full_content": "By continuing to browse our site you agree to our use of cookies, revised Privacy Policy and Terms of Use. You can change your cookie settings through your browser.\nAn AI-plush toy is seen at the Huaqiangbei market in Shenzhen, south China's Guangdong Province, August 12, 2026. /CGTN\nAn AI-plush toy is seen at the Huaqiangbei market in Shenzhen, south China's Guangdong Province, August 12, 2026. /CGTN\nWhen Pallavi Koonjul searched TikTok for places to visit in south China's Shenzhen, one destination kept appearing: Huaqiangbei.\nThe visitor from Mauritius was not there for a conventional souvenir. Walking through one of the world's largest electronics marketplaces, she stopped at a shop selling AI-powered plush toys.\n\"It's very new for me,\" she said, holding up one of the products. \"I'm buying these to try.\"\nNearby, Dylan Brummer, a visitor from South Africa, had a longer shopping list: AI glasses, a laptop and a bigger television for his brother.\nFor a growing number of international visitors, scenes like these are adding another dimension to travel in China. Alongside food, culture and traditional tourist attractions, some are adding electronics markets, technology companies and new consumer gadgets to their itineraries.\nPeople are shopping at the Huaqiang Electronic World, Shenzhen, south China's Guangdong Province, August 12, 2026. /CGTN\nPeople are shopping at the Huaqiang Electronic World, Shenzhen, south China's Guangdong Province, August 12, 2026. /CGTN\nFrom sightseeing to tech shopping\nThe trend comes as China continues to make inbound travel easier.\nOrdinary passport holders from 50 countries can currently enter China without a visa for up to 30 days for purposes including tourism and business under China's unilateral visa-free policy. Separately, nationals of 55 countries are eligible for the country's 240-hour visa-free transit policy, which allows qualified travelers transiting to a third country or region to stay for up to 10 days and engage in tourism and business activities.\nFor Shenzhen, that growing international footfall is increasingly visible in Huaqiangbei, and what brings them here is not necessarily low prices.\nA demonstration of the floating text function of AI glasses is seen at the Huaqiang Electronic World, Shenzhen, south China's Guangdong Province, August 12, 2026. /CGTN\nA demonstration of the floating text function of AI glasses is seen at the Huaqiang Electronic World, Shenzhen, south China's Guangdong Province, August 12, 2026. /CGTN\nBrummer, for example, tried a pair of Chinese AI glasses and was surprised to find that they cost more than Meta's Ray-Ban smart glasses. But what caught his attention was their functionality.\n\"The screen is better, and the translation is way better,\" he said. \"When this gentleman was talking, I could read exactly what he was saying. It was pretty cool.\"\nForeign visitors are seen inside the Huaqiang Electronic World, Shenzhen, south China's Guangdong Province, August 12, 2026. /CGTN\nForeign visitors are seen inside the Huaqiang Electronic World, Shenzhen, south China's Guangdong Province, August 12, 2026. /CGTN\nMore than an electronics market\nHuaqiangbei's appeal is closely tied to its unusual concentration of businesses.\nAccording to the Huaqiangbei Subdistrict Office, some 115,000 business entities and 35 specialized electronics markets are packed into just 1.45 square kilometers, offering more than one million types of electronic components.\nThat density means a visitor can walk from a shop selling finished AI glasses to component suppliers, hardware companies, software developers and exporters within the same commercial district.\nFor entrepreneurs, that can make Huaqiangbei less a shopping mall than a place to turn an idea into a product.\nZhang Guoping, founder and CEO of Shenzhen CHUPAD Electronic Technology Co., has seen that process firsthand. His company was among the early developers of a dual-screen AI translation device, which allows two people speaking different languages to see translated conversations on opposite sides of the screen.\n\"It's really quick to develop a product here,\" Zhang said. \"If you need a prototype or need to match hardware with software, companies might even work overnight to get it done for you.\"\n\"As long as you have the volume and the demand, we'll get it done.\"\nFor Zhang, proximity to suppliers also means products do not remain static. Feedback from overseas buyers can quickly become a new software function or hardware configuration, allowing companies to respond to changing demand without going through lengthy development cycles.\nA promotion of the 'Huaqiangbei Speed' is seen on the facade of an entrepreneurship service center at Huaqiangbei, Shenzhen, south China's Guangdong Province, August 12, 2026. /CGTN\nA promotion of the 'Huaqiangbei Speed' is seen on the facade of an entrepreneurship service center at Huaqiangbei, Shenzhen, south China's Guangdong Province, August 12, 2026. /CGTN\n'Design in the morning, prototype in the afternoon'\nThat speed has long been one of Huaqiangbei's defining characteristics.\n\"Design in the morning, prototype in the afternoon, mass production the next day and global shipment within a week,\" said Xu Jiyue, deputy director of the Party and Administrative Office of Huaqiangbei Subdistrict.\nXu attributes that speed to more than four decades of accumulated electronics expertise, combined with Shenzhen's advanced manufacturing and service sectors.\nThe ecosystem also helps explain why Huaqiangbei continues to draw international business.\nAccording to Xu, that area now records more than 8,000 foreign merchants and visitors from 183 countries and regions on an average day, making international sourcing part of the everyday rhythm of the market.\nThat international role is receiving renewed attention as Shenzhen prepares to host APEC meetings in 2026.The district is upgrading multilingual services, international payment support and visitor facilities to make the market easier for overseas visitors to navigate.",
        "content": "By continuing to browse our site you agree to our use of cookies, revised Privacy Policy and Terms of Use. You can change your cookie settings through your browser.\nAn AI-plush toy is seen at the Huaqiangbei market in Shenzhen, south China's Guangdong Province, August 12, 2026. /CGTN\nAn AI-plush toy is seen at the Huaqiangbei market in Shenzhen, south China's Guangdong Province, August 12, 2026. /...",
