    {
        "title": "Ariana Grande blasts Trump team — again — over music used for social post",
        "link": "https://www.cbc.ca/news/entertainment/ariana-grande-trump-music-social-9.7315673?cmp=rss",
        "source": "CBC | Top Stories News",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T21:38:57.177810+00:00",
        "image": "https://i.cbc.ca/ais/cf3d446e-61bb-41d3-9d22-59dd443b283c,1787330198873/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C187%2C3594%2C2021%29%3BResize%3D620",
        "full_content": "Ariana Grande blasts Trump team — again — over music used for social post\nSinger claps back for second time this summer: 'Never use my music again'\nFor the second time this summer, Ariana Grande is blasting U.S. President Donald Trump's administration for using her music in a social media post.\nThis time around, the Team Trump account added her song We Can't Be Friends (Wait For Your Love) to a TikTok post that even tags the Grammy-winning performer.\nTrump's team uploaded a post Thursday showing clips of the president striding up to a podium to speak, with text overlaid on the video partly quoting from Grande's hit 2024 track. The text reads: \"Me and my truth we sit in silence… men can't have babies and should never compete in women's sports.\"\nThe post's caption added, \"This shouldn't be controversial!\" along with several hashtags, including the singer's name.\nGrande appeared to clap back in the comment section. \"Never use my music again. Also your truth is false,\" read a comment from Grande's verified account.\nSound has since been disengaged on the post and Grande’s comment apparently removed, though audience members have subsequently and repeatedly reposted screenshots of it.\nGrande's curt response comes after the actor and pop star's mid-June criticism of the administration for using her 2024 song Bye to accompany a video — also shared on TikTok — about immigration policy that depicts federal agents arresting and handcuffing people.\n\"Please do not ever use my music in relation to this barbaric, inhumane, heinous nonsense,\" she posted in a comment on that video.\nObjections from Taylor Swift to ABBA\nCurrently closing out her Eternal Sunshine Tour in London, Grande joins a wide-ranging and storied array of performers who've blasted the Trump team for spinning their tracks.\nOver the years, Trump's promotional team and campaigns have run afoul of scores of artists after playing their music at rallies or featuring songs in social videos. Artists ranging from Neil Young, John Fogerty and Queen to the Rolling Stones, ABBA and Beyoncé, and the estates of Leonard Cohen, Prince and Tom Petty have all objected to Trump's use of their music.\nHis team regularly turns to contemporary pop artists in particular: songs from Sabrina Carpenter and Katy Perry were recently included in social media posts. The team also doesn't shy away from playing music from performers Trump has railed against or who've condemned him or his policies, including Drake, Bad Bunny and Taylor Swift.\nSome artists have issued cease and desist orders or filed lawsuits in response. More recently, certain artists — like Swift and Bad Bunny — have moved quickly and quietly to have their music removed from or muted in Team Trump social videos, purportedly through copyright infringement claims.\nWhile political campaigns and others can buy licensing packages from music-rights organizations to legally play songs from vast databases, individual acts may opt out of inclusion for particular reasons. Artists who haven't opted out can also still object.\nWith files from The Associated Press and Reuters",
        "content": "Ariana Grande blasts Trump team — again — over music used for social post\nSinger claps back for second time this summer: 'Never use my music again'\nFor the second time this summer, Ariana Grande is blasting U.S. President Donald Trump's administration for using her music in a social media post.\nThis time around, the Team Trump account added her song We Can't Be Friends (Wait For Your Love) to a Ti...",
