    {
        "title": "Survivor describes moments after plane ripped through his vehicle in Prince George, B.C.",
        "link": "https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/british-columbia/plane-crash-survivor-shares-story-9.7315842?cmp=rss",
        "source": "CBC | Top Stories News",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T21:38:57.177768+00:00",
        "image": "https://i.cbc.ca/ais/1d596da3-d97b-413f-81cd-3b6bc526d57d,1787338526492/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C0%2C1920%2C1080%29%3BResize%3D620",
        "full_content": "Survivor describes moments after plane rips through his vehicle in Prince George\n1 person dead and Transportation Safety Board investigating emergency landing\nSurvivors of a dramatic emergency plane landing in Prince George, B.C., that claimed one life and left several others injured are sharing their stories.\nJaime Pawliuk says he was driving to work on Thursday afternoon when he saw the aircraft barreling toward him.\n\"All of a sudden it was on top of me,\" he said.\n\"I tried to swerve as much as I could to get into the ditch, but unfortunately I was only able to make it just a little ways before the wing just sheared the entire top of my vehicle off and showered me with glass.\n\"I have superficial cuts all in my hands. I have glass still in my hair and, luckily, I was able to walk out of it.\"\nThe plane, a twin-engine Beech King Air 200 aircraft, made its emergency landing just before 2:30 p.m. PT Thursday, landing on Foothills Boulevard, a four-lane road connecting several residential neighbourhoods in Prince George.\nPawliuk and others had to leave their vehicles at the scene while the investigation is underway.\nOn Friday afternoon, several smashed and damaged vehicles were taken away from the scene on the back of flatbed trucks.\nMohit Arora told CBC News he saw a pickup truck get struck by the plane's wing, but the driver survived.\nHe shared photos showing a piece of the plane through the truck's windshield. He also shared images showing a propeller in the middle of the road, and another with a yellow car in a ditch.\n4 vehicles hit: TSB\nThe Transportation Safety Board (TSB), which is leading the investigation, said Friday that it had deployed investigators to document the scene, gather evidence and arrange interviews.\nIt said the aircraft had departed Sturdee Airstrip, a remote gravel airfield in north-central B.C., at about 1:25 p.m. PT with two crew and six passengers aboard.\nThe plane, which was bound for the Prince George International Airport, was forced to make an emergency landing when one of its engines lost power, according to the TSB.\nWhile police had initially said two vehicles were involved in the incident, the TSB said Friday that the aircraft collided with four vehicles on Foothills Boulevard.\nThe plane was contracted and no injuries to those inside the plane have been reported.\nVideo from inside the plane verified by CBC News show at least eight people were in the plane, including two pilots and six passengers. Some of the plane's passengers had to walk away from the scene and call a taxi to be taken home.\nOfficials say 10 people were assessed for injury, with two taken to hospital in stable condition and one person, believed to be in one of the vehicles hit by the plane, is dead.",
