        "title": "US Navy considers sidelining black hero sailor for Trump – media",
        "link": "https://www.rt.com/news/644501-us-navy-trump-carrier/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=RSS",
        "source": "RT - Daily news",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T21:38:57.177314+00:00",
        "image": "https://mf.b37mrtl.ru/files/2026.08/article/6a88a18985f540521e1b6efe.jpg",
        "full_content": "US Navy considers sidelining black hero sailor for Trump – media\nThe US Navy is reportedly considering whether to rename an upcoming aircraft carrier honoring World War II black hero Doris Miller after President Donald Trump in an unprecedented move.\nThe ongoing discussions were first reported on Friday by CNN, citing three sources familiar with the matter. The report was corroborated by CBN News, with two unnamed US officials confirming to the broadcaster that deliberations have been going on.\nThe USS Doris Miller is set to become the fourth Ford-class aircraft carrier and is expected to be commissioned in the mid-2030s. Under the first Trump administration, the US Navy announced the decision to name it after Miller, who distinguished himself during the attack on Pearl Harbor. The sailor, an enlisted cook with no appropriate training, managed to shoot down at least one incoming Japanese aircraft during the attack, becoming the first black recipient of the Navy Cross for his feat.\nThe navy is now reportedly weighing renaming a different warship after Miller, as well as posthumously recommending him for the Medal of Honor, the top US decoration for military valor. The sailor was killed in 1943 in the Japanese torpedoing of the USS Liscome Bay, which remains the deadliest carrier sinking in US Navy history, with over 700 crew lost.\nDuring conversations regarding the ship’s name, the US Navy reportedly stopped referring to the ship as the USS Doris Miller, calling it exclusively by its hull number, CVN-81, one of the sources told CNN. The service has also been reportedly reviewing its rules covering vessel appellation, as naming a carrier after a sitting president would be an unprecedented and highly controversial move.\nA recent Trump executive order, in which he tasked the Pentagon and US Navy to come up with a plan to replace electromagnetic aircraft launch systems on carriers with old-school steam and hydraulic catapults, also referred to the ship only by its hull number.\nThe US president has shown a great interest in shipbuilding during his second term. In December last year, Trump announced plans to construct a ‘Golden Fleet’ of massive, aptly named Trump-class battleships that would be “100 times more powerful” than any ever built. Trump said he approved building two ships to start, with plans for up to 25.\nThe ‘Golden Fleet’ affair reportedly resulted in the downfall of Navy Secretary John Phelan, who was sacked this April for allegedly failing to deliver a plan to build the Trump-class battleships on a “nearly impossible timeline.”",
