        "title": "OpenAI Rolls Out Flexible Codex Pricing for Business and Enterprise Teams",
        "link": "https://dev.to/alifar/openai-rolls-out-flexible-codex-pricing-for-business-and-enterprise-teams-1p3f",
        "source": "DEV Community",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T21:38:57.176963+00:00",
        "image": "https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=1200,height=627,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fscalevise.com%2Fresources%2Fcontent%2Fimages%2F2026%2F08%2Fopenai-codex-flexible-pricing-teams.webp",
        "full_content": "OpenAI has formally rolled out a more flexible way for organizations to buy and use Codex. Eligible ChatGPT Business and Enterprise workspaces can add Codex-only seats on a pay-as-you-go basis, with usage billed through API-style token consumption rather than a fixed fee for each of those seats.\nThe change matters because it separates AI coding access from a conventional per-user subscription model. A team can provision Codex-only members for development work while managing consumption through credits, rather than treating every user as a full ChatGPT seat. OpenAI also reduced the annual ChatGPT Business seat price from $25 to $20 and introduced promotional credits intended to lower the cost of onboarding new Codex users.\nOpenAI details the model in its official flexible Codex pricing announcement for teams. The initial announcement was made on April 2, 2026. A June 24, 2026 update clarified that existing pay-as-you-go seats remain in place, while the availability of new Business pay-as-you-go seats may change.\nHow OpenAI's Codex team pricing works\nThe central change is the availability of Codex-only seats within Business and Enterprise workspaces. These seats have no fixed seat fee. Instead, their Codex consumption is charged using API-style token rates. OpenAI's approach gives organizations a way to make Codex available to users whose primary need is AI-assisted coding, without requiring the same seat model used for standard ChatGPT access.\nThis is not a claim that coding costs are universally lower. A pay-as-you-go model makes expenditure more directly dependent on usage, so the financial effect will vary by team and workload. The documented advantage is billing flexibility: organizations can align Codex costs with token consumption and use credits across supported workspace features where their plan allows it.\n| Item | ChatGPT Business seat | Codex-only seat | \n|---|---|---|\n| Pricing approach | $20 per seat annually, reduced from $25 | Pay as you go through API-style token consumption | \n| Fixed seat fee | Per-seat annual price | No fixed fee for Codex-only seats | \n| Onboarding promotion | Not specified in the announcement | $100 in Codex credits per new member, capped at $500 per team | \n| Workspace context | ChatGPT Business | Eligible ChatGPT Business and Enterprise workspaces | \nShared credits change the purchasing model\nOpenAI's pricing framework also links usage across supported workspace tools. Credits and usage for Codex, ChatGPT Work, ChatGPT for Excel, and related Workspace features can draw from a common pool when that capability is available on the plan. OpenAI Help Center guidance corroborates that credits can work across these supported features and that Codex usage follows API-style token pricing.\nFor administrators, that creates a broader budgeting question than the cost of a single coding tool. The same credit pool can cover several AI-enabled workflows, which may simplify purchasing but also means usage in one feature can affect the credits available to another. Organizations will need to understand which features their plan supports and how they want to allocate credits across development and business use cases.\nThe rollout includes several practical elements:\n- Codex-only members can be added without fixed seat fees, with consumption billed by token usage.\n- New Codex-only team members are eligible for $100 in promotional Codex credits, up to $500 per team.\n- ChatGPT Business annual seats have a documented price reduction from $25 to $20 per seat.\n- Supported workspace tools can use a shared credits model, rather than keeping credits isolated to Codex alone.\n- Existing pay-as-you-go seats remain unaffected by the June update, although new-seat availability for Business may change.\nWhat developers and enterprise teams should watch\nFor developers, Codex-only seats could make it easier for a company to extend AI coding access to targeted groups. The operational trade-off is that usage-based billing requires closer attention to consumption than a simple fixed-seat model. Teams should account for token use as part of their engineering-tool budget rather than assuming a predictable monthly cost per user.\nFor Business and Enterprise administrators, the shared-credit design is equally important. Codex spending cannot be viewed entirely in isolation if credits are also used for ChatGPT Work, Excel, or related features. The relevant question becomes how a workspace governs access and usage across its supported AI tools.\nOpenAI's June 24 clarification also makes availability worth monitoring. The company states that existing pay-as-you-go seats are unaffected, but it notes that new pay-as-you-go seats for Business may be subject to changing availability. Organizations considering the model should therefore confirm the options visible in their own workspace before planning a broader rollout.\nFor organizations evaluating AI coding access, the practical issue is not simply token cost. It is how shared credits, workspace permissions, and developer workflows fit into governance and budgeting. Scalevise helps teams map AI tooling to measurable operating needs and implementation controls through its AI consultancy services. Request a consultation to assess a Codex and ChatGPT workspace approach before expanding usage.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat is OpenAI's pay-as-you-go Codex pricing for teams?\nIt is a pricing model for Codex-only seats in eligible ChatGPT Business and Enterprise workspaces. Those seats have no fixed fee, and Codex usage is billed through API-style token consumption.\nWhich workspaces can use Codex-only pay-as-you-go seats?\nOpenAI's rollout targets eligible ChatGPT Business and Enterprise workspaces. The company has also said that availability of new pay-as-you-go seats for Business may change, while existing seats remain unaffected.\nHow does the Codex promotional credit offer work?\nOpenAI offers $100 in Codex credits for each new Codex-only team member. The promotion is capped at $500 per team.\nAre Codex credits separate from ChatGPT Work and ChatGPT for Excel credits?\nWhere supported by an eligible plan, credits and usage for Codex, ChatGPT Work, ChatGPT for Excel, and related Workspace features can draw from a common pool.\nDid OpenAI change ChatGPT Business pricing?\nYes. OpenAI documented a reduction in the annual ChatGPT Business seat price from $25 to $20 per seat.\nConclusion\nOpenAI's flexible Codex pricing gives eligible Business and Enterprise workspaces a consumption-based option for AI coding access, while linking supported AI tools through shared credits. The lower ChatGPT Business seat price and limited onboarding credits add incentives, but the value of the model will depend on how each organization manages usage, access, and the availability of new seats.",
        "content": "OpenAI has formally rolled out a more flexible way for organizations to buy and use Codex. Eligible ChatGPT Business and Enterprise workspaces can add Codex-only seats on a pay-as-you-go basis, with usage billed through API-style token consumption rather than a fixed fee for each of those seats.\nThe change matters because it separates AI coding access from a conventional per-user subscription mode...",
