        "title": "79% of the Poor Believe Wealth Comes From Random Luck. 84% of the Self-Made Rich Prove Them Wrong",
        "link": "https://www.savingadvice.com/articles/2026/08/20/10744843_79-of-the-poor-believe-wealth-comes-from-random-luck-84-of-the-self-made-rich-prove-them-wrong.html",
        "source": "SavingAdvice.com Blog",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T21:05:23.145219+00:00",
        "image": "https://etyb2wffn3d.exactdn.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/79-of-the-Poor-Believe-Wealth-Comes-From-Random-Luck.jpg",
        "full_content": "If you find value in these articles, please share them with your inner circle and encourage them to Sign Up for my Rich Habits Daily Tips/Articles. No one succeeds on their own. Thank You!\n TOM@RICHHABITS.NET\nIn my five-year Rich Habits Study, I interviewed 233 wealthy individuals and 128 people living in poverty. One of the clearest divides in mindset appeared when I asked about the role of luck in building wealth.\nSeventy-nine percent of the poor believed wealth comes from random good luck. Only 8% of the overall rich group agreed. Among the self-made millionaires—the 177 men and women who built their wealth from middle-class or poor beginnings—that number fell even lower. Instead, 84% of the rich (and 97% of the self-made) stated that good daily habits create opportunity luck.\nI have shared this finding in articles on richhabits.net and through media coverage in the United States and 27 other countries, including major outlets such as CBS, Fox News, CNN, and USA Today. The numbers consistently provoke strong reactions because they challenge a deeply held belief: that financial success is mostly a matter of chance.\nWealth Is Created Through Repeated Actions\nThe self-made millionaires in my study did not wait for luck to strike. They created it through repeated actions. They saved at least 10% of their income. They read educational material every day. They exercised regularly. They set written goals and pursued them for years. They networked deliberately, sought mentors, and went beyond what their jobs required. Many failed in business at least once and still continued. Over time those habits produced the opportunities that outsiders later called “luck.\nThe poor group in the study held the opposite view. Nearly 80% attributed wealth to random fortune. That belief correlated with very different daily choices: higher rates of lottery play, limited educational reading, lower savings, and greater credit-card debt. When you believe success is mostly random, the disciplined behaviors that actually generate opportunity feel pointless.\nMy research documented more than 300 categories of behavior, thinking, choices and decisions.\nThe luck question is important because it underlies two different ways of thinking:\nRich Thinking- The belief that you are the architect of your life and\n- Poor Thinking – The myth that you are a victim or circumstances.\n- Rich Thinking – The reality that your daily habits, positive outlook, decision-making and choices create your life circumstances.\nThe self-made rich treated habits as the primary driver for changing their life to create better outcomes. They rejected the idea that wealth is accidental. Zero percent of the self-made group believed wealth is accidental.\nSeventy-nine percent of the poor do nothing, believing luck is outside their control. Ninety-seven percent of the self-made take action, knowing luck is created by their Rich Habits.\nThe data shows that those who view wealth as random luck tend to remain where they are – stuck in poverty or the middle-class.\nConversely, those who forge daily Rich Habits steadily move forward, improving their lives and financial circumstances.\nTom Corley is an accountant, financial planner, public speaker, and author of the books “Effort-Less Wealth: Smart Money Habits At Every Stage of Your Life” and “RichKids: How to Raise Our Children to Be Happy and Successful in Life“. Corley’s work has appeared on CNN, USA Today, The Huffington Post, SUCCESS Magazine, and many other media outlets and podcasts in the U.S. and 27 other countries. Tom is a frequent contributor to Business Insider and CNBC.",
        "content": "If you find value in these articles, please share them with your inner circle and encourage them to Sign Up for my Rich Habits Daily Tips/Articles. No one succeeds on their own. Thank You!\n TOM@RICHHABITS.NET\nIn my five-year Rich Habits Study, I interviewed 233 wealthy individuals and 128 people living in poverty. One of the clearest divides in mindset appeared when I asked about the role of luck ...",
