        "title": "Karnataka Assembly clears bill to help out B khata property owners",
        "link": "https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/politics-and-nation/karnataka-assembly-clears-bill-to-help-out-b-khata-property-owners/articleshow/133402916.cms",
        "source": "Politics-News-Economic Times",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T21:05:23.144900+00:00",
        "image": "https://img.etimg.com/thumb/msid-133402964,width-1200,height-630,imgsize-89522,overlay-economictimes/articleshow.jpg",
        "full_content": "Listen to this article in summarized format\nThe Karnataka Municipal Corporations (Amendment) Bill, 2026, provides for eligible plots and houses in unauthorised layouts to be converted from B khata to A khata after roads in such layouts are formally recognised as public roads.\nIntroducing the Bill, Bengaluru Development Minister Krishna Byre Gowda said the measure was part of the government’s commitment to provide secure and legally recognised ownership, which he described as a “Land Guarantee”.\nThe amendment to Section 284 of the Karnataka Municipal Corporations Act seeks to address problems arising from unauthorised layouts that came up across urban Karnataka over the past three to four decades.\nMany such layouts were developed without adequate roads, drainage, electricity, drinking water and other basic infrastructure, leaving property owners with B khata records and limited access to formal property rights and approvals.\nB khata to A khata\nUnder the amendment, roads earmarked in eligible unauthorised layouts can be declared public roads and formally recorded as such by the municipal body concerned.\nOnce the roads are recognised, eligible property owners can apply for A khata, subject to prescribed rules and conditions.\nThe move is expected to improve access to bank loans and ease hurdles in obtaining building plan approvals and Occupancy Certificates, subject to statutory requirements.\nByre Gowda said the government had in 2024 decided to stop the creation of new unauthorised layouts while finding a solution for properties in legacy layouts. “Ownership can have real security only when property owners are able to obtain A khata,” he said.\nThe government said the system, already implemented in Bengaluru, would now be extended to municipal corporations, municipalities and Gram Panchayats across Karnataka.\nThe legislation also seeks to ensure that the relief for legacy properties does not become a route for creating new unauthorised layouts.",
