        "title": "Pakistan ex-PM Imran Khan taken back to jail after brief hospital visit",
        "link": "https://scroll.in/latest/1095183/pakistans-ex-pm-imran-khan-taken-back-to-jail-after-brief-hospital-visit?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=public",
        "source": "Scroll.in",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T21:05:23.144649+00:00",
        "image": "https://sc0.blr1.cdn.digitaloceanspaces.com/book/190518-fdljjrqizq-1684942655.jpg",
        "full_content": "Pakistan ex-PM Imran Khan taken back to jail after brief hospital visit\nKhan’s family and political party have been raising concerns about his health and poor incarceration conditions.\nPakistan’s former Prime Minister Imran Khan was on Friday shifted back to prison after a brief visit to an Islamabad hospital, Information Minister Attaullah Tarar said.\nHe had been taken to the hospital for a medical examination in compliance with an order by the country’s Supreme Court.\nOn Tuesday, the court had directed the authorities to take Khan to hospital within 48 hours. He was expected to remain in hospital till September 16, according to the order.\nHowever, he was shifted back to Adiala jail on Friday itself.\nThe court’s directive had met a longstanding demand by Khan’s party and his family who have been raising concerns about his health and poor incarceration conditions.\nKhan’s son Kasim had said in February that the family has been informed that the former prime minister has “lost most of the vision in his right eye, with reports indicating only 15% eyesight remains”.\nKhan, founder of the Pakistan Tehreek-e-Insaf, was taken to the Pakistan Institute of Medical Sciences instead of a private health facility because of a “security situation created by [his party] workers”, Tarar claimed on social media.\nThe 73-year-old cricketer-turned-politician was arrested in August 2023 and has been in Rawalpindi’s Adiala jail since September 2023. He is facing several criminal cases, mostly linked to alleged corruption.\nThe Supreme Court order came after a medical board report submitted to the court recommended frequent meetings between Khan and his family to help manage his anxiety and blood pressure.\nThe court also granted Khan’s personal physician permission to meet the former prime minister. However, it directed Khan’s family and lawyers not to share his medical report with the public.\nAdditional Attorney General Aamir Rehman, representing the government, opposed the court order. He said at the time that no notice had been issued to the government and that Khan could not be shifted to hospital immediately.\nKhan formed the Pakistan Tehreek-e-Insaf party in 1996 after retiring from the sport and served as the prime minister between August 2018 and April 2022. His government collapsed after his relations with the Pakistani military leadership deteriorated.\nIn January 2025, a court sentenced Khan to 14 years of imprisonment and his wife Bushra Bibi to seven years after finding them guilty in a corruption case.\nAmnesty International had in December urged the Pakistani authorities to “immediately restore” Khan’s access to his family and legal counsel in line with international human rights law.\nThis came less than a week after the United Nations’ special rapporteur on torture called on the Pakistani government to take immediate steps to address reports of “inhumane and undignified detention conditions” of Khan.\nThe special rapporteur warned that the conditions Khan was reportedly living in “could amount to torture”.\nKhan’s friends from across the world, including former Indian cricketers, have been urging the authorities in Pakistan to treaty him with dignity.\nWritten by Edited by Tanya Shrivastava. Edited by Nachiket Deuskar.\nAlso read: In Pakistan, Prisoner No 804 seems to be everywhere",
