        "full_content": "Danes, in particular young Danes, often use English words in their Danish speech, even when there is a perfectly valid Danish alternative.\nIn an interview with public broadcaster DR, 18-year-old student Julian Norden Søndergaard told the broadcaster that he often uses an English word if he can't find the Danish alternative, and that his vocabulary is larger in English.\n\"That worries me, and that's where the issue lies,\" Stampe told DR. \"We shouldn't blame young people, but as people who are a bit older we have a responsibility to teach young people the Danish language and old Danish phrases and idioms.\"\nStampe does not only want to carry out the package of language laws put forward by the previous government to combat foreign loan words, but also go further, by taking measures to protect Danish from the rising use of AI tools.\n\"I can't be the only one who has noticed the internet and opinion pages overflowing with very long and well-considered articles, where you start to wonder how many of them were written using AI.\"\n\"Are we really seeing linguistic impoverishment take place through these very well-formulated posts? Because when you get a machine to formulate something for you, are you learning to do that yourself? That's something I'm paying close attention to,\" she told Ritzau.\nThe original package of language laws put forward by former culture minister Jakob Engel-Schmidt in February, sprogpakke 1, included giving the Danish Language Council a more active role in protecting the language. That law was not passed before the election in March, but Stampe wants to move forwards with it with some new additions.\nOne of those additions is a new media agreement for broadcasters DR, TV2 and TV2 news with an emphasis on \"strengthening and protecting the Danish language\".\nAccording to Stampe, the issue of English loanwords is not just a problem affecting younger Danes.\n\"A middle-aged woman like me sometimes has to search for the Danish word, wanting to use a word like deale or blame, instead of the Danish alternative,\" she said.\n\"I think that's a common problem, especially in spoken language.\"",
