        "full_content": "Just 407 owned apartments (ejerlejligheder, as opposed to the cheaper andelsboliger) were sold in Copenhagen Municipality in July, which is 24 percent lower than the amount sold in June and 40 percent lower than the amount sold in July 2025.\nBergmann told the Ritzau news wire that usual monthly sale figures for owned apartments are around 400 to 600.\nWhen a significantly higher than average number of apartments are sold, prices usually rise steeply, she said, with the reverse true if fewer than around 400 properties are sold.\n\"With 407 in July the Copenhagen property market for apartments is balancing very close to the level where things historically have started to flash red,\" she said.\nRecent property price figures from Boligsiden seem to support this idea, showing that the average sales price for a Copenhagen apartment did not rise between June and July.\nThat's not only the case in Copenhagen – according to the site's statistics, the number of sold apartments has fallen by 22 percent since July last year.\nBirgit Daetz, head of communications and property economist at Boligsiden, said that demand for apartments is not as high as it has been in the past, particularly in the capital.\n\"We're seeing that in the figures now, fewer apartments are being sold, supply is growing and prices are starting to plateau. That's all pointing towards less demand for apartments,\" she said.",
