        "title": "What's in the Denmark Democrats' new 'Danish values' proposals for schools?",
        "link": "https://www.thelocal.dk/20260820/whats-in-the-denmark-democrats-new-danish-values-proposals-for-schools",
        "source": "The Local Denmark",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T21:05:23.144499+00:00",
        "image": "https://assets.thelocal.com/cdn-cgi/rs:fit:1200/quality:75/plain/https://apiwp.thelocal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/watermarks-logo-20260820-123628-L-1920x1279web.jpg@webp",
        "full_content": "What has she proposed?\nStøjberg has been outspoken in the past about her plans to enforce Danish values at Danish schools. In an interview with public broadcaster DR, she revealed a number of new proposals on this topic.\nThe first is enforcing handshakes with the school principal or another member of school leadership on the first day of school. Hand shakes must, she said, be given to a person of the opposite sex.\nShe also plans on banning students from speaking any language other than Danish. When asked whether this meant that two people speaking English to one another during their lunch break could be kicked out, she replied: “Yes, I think they should. But we might as well be honest that it's not English that's the problem. It's Arabic.”\nStøjberg is also suggesting that all new students starting an upper secondary education in Denmark should be required to sign a contract agreeing to comply with Danish values, as well as urging classmates to keep an eye on each other to ensure that everyone complies with those values.\nWhen asked what values she would highlight in the contract, she mentioned equality, being allowed to have a girlfriend or boyfriend of one’s own choice, and not exercise negative social control.\n“I hope to create an open-minded school, where you have the opportunity to be who you want to be, and where you are not forced to wear a headscarf, and you are not forced to fast during Ramadan,” she added.\nWhat happens if you don’t comply with these rules?\nUnder the proposal, those who choose not to comply with the proposed rules, or those caught breaking the rules, would risk expulsion from school.\nWhy is she proposing this?\nThe proposals, she said, are an effort to eliminate social control and \"dominance culture\", which she claims have been an issue at some Danish upper secondary schools.\nIn addition to that the Muslim organisation known as Hizb ut-Tahrir has made the headlines in Denmark in recent weeks after it sent messages to secondary schools across the country calling for Muslim high school students to \"stand firm in [their] Islamic identity and wear it with pride.”\n\"Many of you will experience a youth environment where behaviour, norms, and lifestyle choices which are not in harmony with Islam are presented as the most natural or desirable choice,\" the letter read, stating that students may experience \"pressure to conform to a culture where following one's desires and cultivating an often shameless and limitless youth culture is presented as normal and valuable, and where noble Islamic values are presented as old-fashioned, limiting or foreign.\"\nStøjberg claims that her proposal is to outline some completely fundamental ground rules, namely that women and men, girls and boys are equal.\nWhat has the reaction been?\nStøjberg’s proposal has received criticism, both from school principals in The Danish Association of Upper Secondary Schools and from high school students from The Union of Danish Upper Secondary School Students.\nThe chairperson from The Danish Association of Upper Secondary Schools, Maja Bødtcher-Hansen, told DR that she fundamentally shares the concern regarding negative social control and antidemocratic ideology and believes that something needs to be done. However, she doesn’t think Støjberg's proposals involving orders, rules and injunctions are the solution.\nThe high school students’ chairperson, Oscar Tønsberg Hoffmann, told DR that Støjberg’s proposals are a clear example of tokenism, which is unlikely to work.\n“It is clear that this proposal is about nothing more than making life miserable for Muslim high school students,” he said.\n“We just think they should be allowed to be high school students,” he added.\nWhat’s the status of this proposal?\nThe proposal is due to be formally put forward on Thursday afternoon. Once put forward, it will be discussed in parliament.\nAs this is a proposal by one of the smaller parties in the opposition, Støjberg is unlikely to get the backing needed for it to become law.",
        "content": "What has she proposed?\nStøjberg has been outspoken in the past about her plans to enforce Danish values at Danish schools. In an interview with public broadcaster DR, she revealed a number of new proposals on this topic.\nThe first is enforcing handshakes with the school principal or another member of school leadership on the first day of school. Hand shakes must, she said, be given to a person of ...",
