        "title": "Danish households more positive about the economy",
        "link": "https://www.thelocal.dk/20260821/danish-households-more-positive-about-the-economy",
        "source": "The Local Denmark",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T21:05:23.144489+00:00",
        "image": "https://assets.thelocal.com/cdn-cgi/rs:fit:1200/quality:75/plain/https://apiwp.thelocal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/watermarks-logo-20250724-102648-A-1920x1280web.jpg@webp",
        "full_content": "The new data, from a monthly questionnaire by Statistics Denmark completed by a representative sample of Danish adults, asks questions regarding family, personal finances and the national economy, which are then used to create a “consumer trust indicator” figure.\nThe questionnaire answers are translated to numbers. Positive numbers indicate a positive outlook on the economy, whereas negative numbers represent negative expectations.\nAlthough consumer trust is still negative, August’s numbers have taken a step in the right direction.\nAugust's consumer trust indicator was at minus 13.1, slightly up from July which was minus 14.7, and significantly above the 6-month average which was minus 15.7.\nThis improvement is due to a more positive view on family finances, as well as Denmark’s economy today.\nHowever the expectation for the Danish economy in a year's time has fallen.\nWhen consumers were asked for their view on the possibility of saving up money, their answer was 33.2 compared to 29.9 the previous month.\n“This combination - widespread uncertainty and concern about the future economic situation on the one side, and a positive view on being able to save up on the other - can contribute to a reluctance to spend money, with Danish consumers prioritising saving up money instead,” said Helle Dalsgaard, Head of Counselling at Danish pension fund Sampension.\nDespite this, August’s numbers are overall the highest since March 2022.\nThe “consumer trust indicator” number has been negative since November 2021.\nKristian Skriver, Head of Macroeconomics at Dansk Erhverv, an association that represents thousands of companies in Denmark, explains that this is likely due to the spike in the price of gas, fuel and electricity which happened at that time.\n“Since then, consumers have never been given the opportunity to get a foothold before the next economic challenges hit them,” he explained.\nWith reporting from Ritzau",
        "content": "The new data, from a monthly questionnaire by Statistics Denmark completed by a representative sample of Danish adults, asks questions regarding family, personal finances and the national economy, which are then used to create a “consumer trust indicator” figure.\nThe questionnaire answers are translated to numbers. Positive numbers indicate a positive outlook on the economy, whereas negative numbe...",
