        "title": "Social Democrats open to making Danish citizenship provisional for 5 years",
        "link": "https://www.thelocal.dk/20260821/social-democrats-open-to-making-danish-citizenship-provisional-for-5-years",
        "source": "The Local Denmark",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T21:05:23.144485+00:00",
        "image": "https://assets.thelocal.com/cdn-cgi/rs:fit:1200/quality:75/plain/https://apiwp.thelocal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/watermarks-logo-20240908-191447-L-1920x1279web.jpg@webp",
        "full_content": "The Liberals' new proposal would see anyone granted Danish citizenship facing the possibility of it being stripped from them if they commit a serious crime within five years of the application being granted.\nA serious crime is defined in the proposal as a crime that could result in a prison sentence.\nUnder the plan those foreigners who lose their Danish citizenship would also be barred from applying for Danish citizenship in the future, the party's chair, Troels Lund Poulsen, told TV 2 news.\n\"If you get Danish citizenship, you should behave yourself. We've seen examples of citizenships, which we cannot withdraw, being granted to people who commit crimes against Danish society. That's the challenge today and that's why we're proposing this model,\" Poulsen told TV 2.\nThe far-right Denmark Democrats has already proposed a similar policy earlier this year albeit with a ten-year provisional period. Poulsen's party was in government at the time but rejected that proposal.\nPoulsen now says that a five year provisional period is \"reasonable\".\nThe Social Democrats, who were part of the previous government alongside the Liberals, and who are the largest party in the current government, have confirmed they open to Poulsen's proposal.\nSocial Democrat citizenship spokesperson Katrine Evelyn Jensen told P1 Morgen radio that the idea is \"not alien\" to her or her party.\n\"I don't want to say yes or no,\" she said. \"\n\"We think it should be difficult to get citizenship. Right now, I'm more concerned with making sure we weed out the people with anti-democratic views,\" she said.\nANALYSIS: What are the chances of Danish citizenship being made 'provisional'?\n\"I'm also very concerned with the fact that people should not commit crimes, which is what the proposal is about. I'm open to all methods we can bring to the table.\"\nThe government is by no means in agreement on the issue. Magnus Georg Jensen, immigration and integration spokesperson for the Social Liberals, who are one of the four parties in the Danish government, told public broadcaster DR that the proposal is \"completely out of proportion\".\n\"I think we're focusing on a very, very small problem. Since 2015, only 0.17 percent of people who have been granted citizenship in their own right have committed crimes which have resulted in a prison sentence,\" he said.\n\"That's why I think it's completely out of proportion and an extremely tiny issue the Liberals are trying to solve.\"\nJensen said that he agrees that it should be difficult to become a Danish citizen, but not that citizenship should be provisional.\n\"If you have committed a crime after fulfilling these requirements [for citizenship], then you should be punished. You're a criminal. But if you have citizenship, you should be punished as a Danish citizen.\"\nThe provisional citizenship proposal is currently far from becoming law, as it hasn't even been put forward in parliament yet, rather it is just a proposal made by the Liberals at their summer meeting.\nIt does, however, look like it could become a contentious issue, as the Liberals and Social Democrats are in favour. If they can get enough other parties on side, it could potentially be approved in parliament despite not having the support of all four governmental parties.",
        "content": "The Liberals' new proposal would see anyone granted Danish citizenship facing the possibility of it being stripped from them if they commit a serious crime within five years of the application being granted.\nA serious crime is defined in the proposal as a crime that could result in a prison sentence.\nUnder the plan those foreigners who lose their Danish citizenship would also be barred from applyi...",
