    {
        "title": "ANALYSIS: What are the chances of Danish citizenship being made 'provisional'?",
        "link": "https://www.thelocal.dk/20260821/analysis-what-are-the-chances-of-danish-citizenship-becoming-provisional",
        "source": "The Local Denmark",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T21:05:23.144475+00:00",
        "image": "https://assets.thelocal.com/cdn-cgi/rs:fit:1200/quality:75/plain/https://apiwp.thelocal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/watermarks-logo-20240908-191339-L-1920x1279web.jpg@webp",
        "full_content": "What has been proposed?\nDenmark's opposition Liberal party has called for new citizenships to be made provisional for five years, and the Social Democrats, the largest party in the Danish government, has said it is open to the move.\nWhat exactly does provisional mean?\nUnder the proposal, anyone granted Danish citizenship would face the possibility of it being stripped from them if they commit a serious crime within five years of the application being granted.\nA serious crime is defined as a crime that could result in a prison sentence.\nUnder the plan those foreigners who lose their Danish citizenship would also be barred from applying for Danish citizenship in the future, Liberal leader Troels Lund Poulsen told TV 2 news.\n\"If you get Danish citizenship, you should behave yourself. We've seen examples of citizenships, which we cannot withdraw, being granted to people who commit crimes against Danish society. That's the challenge today and that's why we're proposing this model,\" Poulsen told the broadcaster.\nIs this a new proposal?\nNot really. The far-right Denmark Democrats proposed a similar policy earlier this year albeit with a ten-year provisional period. Poulsen's party was in government at the time but rejected that proposal.\nIn the run-up to the Danish election this spring, Poulsen's party instead proposed a five-year transitional period, and on Friday Poulsen said that the question of provisional citizenship was an important topic during the brief period where he was tasked with forming a government after the election.\nOf course, parties say a lot of things when they are campaigning for elections, and not all of those things are brought up again once the new government takes over. This is the first time Poulsen has re-proposed the measure since the new centre-left government, which his party is not part of, took over.\nHow have the other parties reacted?\nThe Social Democrats, who were part of the previous government alongside the Liberals, and who are the largest party in the current government, have confirmed they open to Poulsen's proposal.\nSocial Democrat citizenship spokesperson Katrine Evelyn Jensen told P1 Morgen radio that the idea is \"not alien\" to her or her party.\n\"I don't want to say yes or no,\" she said. \"We think it should be difficult to get citizenship. Right now, I'm more concerned with making sure we weed out the people with anti-democratic views.\"\nThe Social Democrats have also said they are in favour of introducing interviews of prospective citizenship candidates to ensure they do not hold anti-Danish, anti-Democratic views.\nWhat about the other parties in the government?\nThe governmental party which has been most outspoken against the proposal so far is the Social Liberals. Magnus Georg Jensen, immigration and integration spokesperson for the party, told public broadcaster DR that the proposal is \"completely out of proportion\".\n\"I think we're focusing on a very, very small problem. Since 2015, only 0.17 percent of people who have been granted citizenship in their own right have committed crimes which have resulted in a prison sentence,\" he said.\n\"That's why I think it's completely out of proportion and an extremely tiny issue the Liberals are trying to solve.\"\nJensen said that he agrees that it should be difficult to become a Danish citizen, but not that citizenship should be provisional.\n\"If you have committed a crime after fulfilling these requirements [for citizenship], then you should be punished. You're a criminal. But if you have citizenship, you should be punished as a Danish citizen.\"\nWhy are they proposing this now?\nFirstly, because the Liberals want this to become law. The second, perhaps more strategic reason, is that the Liberals know this is an issue which the government disagrees on (and which the right-wing opposition appears to agree on, albeit to differing degrees), and they want to make the government look weak and divided.\nHow likely is it that this could become law?\nThe Liberals, along with the Moderates, ruled alongside the Social Democrats prior to the election, so they know this is an issue the Social Democrats approve of.\nThe Social Democrats are the largest party in parliament, with 38 seats. The Liberals have 18 seats, giving them 56 of the 90 needed to get a law through parliament. The rest of the so-called 'blue block', made up of the Denmark Democrats, Danish People's Party, Liberal Alliance, Citizens Party and Conservatives have 55 seats together, so the Social Democrats and Liberals wouldn't even need all of the opposition on their side to get the law through.\nHowever, if they do this, the other governing parties will not be happy. Now, the Social Democrats will have to decide whether it is more important to them that this proposal goes through, or more important to show a united front with their government allies.\nHow could this affect people who have already applied?\nThere is currently no discussion around transitional rules for people who are already in the citizenship queue, and it appears as if the government (or at least the Social Democrats) is happy to delay pending citizenship applications for even longer, until the new rules come into force.\nFor that reason, it looks currently as if people already in the queue for citizenship could be affected by this new proposal if it comes into force, but it's impossible to say for certain until a formal proposal has been put forward.\nIs this the only thing the government is in disagreement on?\nNo, there are more citizenship-related issues which are causing problems among the four governing parties.\nThe other two parties in the government, the Moderates and Green Left, have together with the Social Liberals criticised the Social Democrats for leaving the more than 2,000 people whose citizenship applications have been paused since February in limbo.\nMagnus Georg Jensen, the Social Liberals' immigration and integration spokesperson, described it as \"unreasonable\".\n\"They've met the requirements which have previously been part of the citizenship agreement and for that reason I don't think it's worthy for a democracy and a state where the rule-of-law is applied that these people are being left in a huge vacuum,\" he told public broadcaster DR.\nCitizenship applications are approved by the Danish parliament, meaning that each applicant's name has to be included on a citizenship bill passed in parliament before they become citizens.\nApplications were put on pause before the election in part due to discussions as to whether interviews should be put in place to screen prospective citizens to see if they hold \"Danish and democratic\" views.\nThe Social Democrats' citizenship spokesperson, Katrine Evelyn Jensen, has said she wants to investigate the possibilities for introducing a screening system before processing of applications can be resumed. She also rejected the other parties' claims that the situation is unreasonable.\n\"I think it's fair that we are looking into whether we can introduce this screening model,\" she told DR.",
        "content": "What has been proposed?\nDenmark's opposition Liberal party has called for new citizenships to be made provisional for five years, and the Social Democrats, the largest party in the Danish government, has said it is open to the move.\nWhat exactly does provisional mean?\nUnder the proposal, anyone granted Danish citizenship would face the possibility of it being stripped from them if they commit a se...",
