        "title": "Music Industry Moves: My Morning Jacket and Jim James Sign Publishing Deal With Peermusic",
        "link": "https://variety.com/2026/music/news/iheartmedias-alissa-pollack-president-mediabase-1236836639/",
        "source": "Variety",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T21:05:23.143360+00:00",
        "image": "https://variety.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/JIM-JAMES-MAIN-PRESS-PHOTO_credit_DannyClinch_8x10-e1787340809291.jpg?w=1000&h=563&crop=1",
        "full_content": "Peermusic has signed a worldwide publishing deal with My Morning Jacket and frontman Jim James, as announced by Jamie Cerreta, President of U.S. and Canada. Through the deal, Peermusic will publish the go-forward works of My Morning Jacket as well as Jim James’ go-forward solo recordings, starting with his new album “Wowed Out,” due out August 28 on ATO Records. The deal marks a decades-long publishing relationship between My Morning Jacket, Jim James, and Cerreta, who first signed My Morning Jacket and Jim James to a publishing deal with Chrysalis in 2002.\nJames said, “Since 2002, Jamie Cerreta and I have worked together building an incredible musical connection and friendship. He has always been such a wonderful guide and protector for all of the music I have created over the years, and I am so excited to join him and the rest of the family over at Peermusic!”\nPopular on Variety\nCerreta added, “I have been so lucky to work with Jim for most of my career. It has been a true honor to represent his amazing songwriting throughout the years. I couldn’t be more thrilled that he has joined up with what we are building at Peermusic.”\n+ SoundExchange has awarded MC Lyte with the SoundExchange Music Fairness Award. The honor recognizes the pioneering hip-hop artist’s legacy of advocacy, including her efforts to raise awareness, engage members of Congress, and urge passage of the American Music Fairness Act.\nLyte was inducted into the Rock & Roll Hall of Fame in 2026 in the organization’s Early Influence category.\nIn 2024, MC Lyte joined DMC (Run-DMC’s Darryl McDaniels) and Melle Mel to visit Capitol Hill to meet with key lawmakers about the American Music Fairness Act and urge its passage to compel corporate terrestrial radio broadcasters to pay performance royalties. She reengaged with legislators again this summer, visiting members of Congress to continue to press for passage of AMFA.\n“MC Lyte has spent her career breaking barriers, elevating artists, and using her voice to drive meaningful change,” said Michael Huppe, President and CEO of SoundExchange. “We are proud to honor her with the SoundExchange Music Fairness Award in recognition of her enduring leadership and advocacy for the creators whose work powers our culture.”\n“I am deeply grateful to SoundExchange for this recognition and for its unwavering commitment to standing up for artists,” said MC Lyte. “Advocacy has always been part of my purpose, and I believe that creators deserve to be respected, protected, and fairly compensated for the music and culture they bring to the world. I’m proud to stand with SoundExchange in the fight for music fairness and for a future where every artist’s work is valued.”\n+ 10th Street Entertainment management company has expanded with the official launch of the Pub Crawl Recordings label group and Pub Crawl Songs music publishing company. These new ventures will be an expansion on its current artist management division, dedicated to comprehensive artist development, curation, and maximizing creative IP.\nLeading this expansion, 10th Street Entertainment’s Chris Nilsson (President) and Lewis Kovac (COO) have appointed veteran music industry executive Piero Giramonti as Executive Vice President of Operations and Artist Manager. Giramonti brings decades of top-tier industry leadership to the role, having previously served as President of Sumerian Records and General Manager of Capitol Music Group’s Harvest Records and Caroline Independent Distribution.\n“I am so happy to be joining an organization with the pedigree of 10th Street Entertainment, where artists come first, and an incredible staff operate with passion, and with complete transparency and integrity,” says Giramonti. “I have known Lewis Kovac for 30 years. We have always had stellar success and great times working together. I have only recently gotten to know Chris Nilsson, but I can already see that he has passion, is thoughtful, calm, highly knowledgeable and very cool.”\n“Chris and I have always wanted to expand our company’s growth organically and we had the opportunity to reconnect with Piero at the right time,” says Kovac. “We share so many passions and our dedicated team is deserving of global scale to give our artists a world stage. Working with The Orchard is already smooth and they are excited to start releasing music.”\nJoining 10th Street’s Nashville office are Elizabeth Brahan Wright (VP of Music Publishing/Artist Manager), Clint Perry (Artist Manager) and Jim Prisco (Artist Manager). The team manages the careers of Tyler Farr and Kanaan Brock, as well as Morgan Wade.\n+ Country artist Alison Nichols has signed a global publishing deal with BMG, expanding her relationship with the company where she is also signed as a recording artist with BBR Music Group/BMG Nashville. As an Asian-American artist in the country format, Nichols proudly describes herself as “half Korean, half redneck, and 100% American.”\n“Signing my first publishing deal is something I’ve dreamed about for a long time, and I’m so thankful to be doing it with the BMG family. Having people like Amy who believed in me and my songwriting from the very beginning makes this moment even more meaningful, and I can’t wait to see where this next chapter leads.”\n“We’re thrilled to continue building alongside Alison as she enters this next chapter of her career,” says Amy Scott, Director, Creative BMG Publishing. “As both an artist and songwriter, she brings a rare combination of authenticity, passion and humor to everything she creates. Her ability to tell honest stories in a way that feels both deeply personal and universally relatable is what makes her such a special talent, and we’re excited to help champion her songwriting for years to come.”\n+ Electric Feel Publishing, part of Electric Feel Entertainment management, has signed the Elements (Keven Wolfsohn and Paul Bogumil Goller).The duo executive-produced Afrobeats superstar Ayra Starr’s new album, “Starr Girl,” and over the years have collaborated with Burna Boy, Pop Smoke, Rema, Wizkid, Central Cee, Zara Larsson, Lewis Capaldi and more.\n“We’re grateful to the EF team for their support and vision, and we’re excited to build the next chapter of our sound together. Music has always been about pushing boundaries and creating records that connect with people around the world. Joining Electric Feel Entertainment gives us an incredible team to help take that vision even further” said the Elements in a statement.\n“We’re excited to welcome The Elements to Electric Feel Publishing. Bogo and Kev have built an incredible track record and have become trusted collaborators for some of the biggest artists in the world. We’re looking forward to working with them and supporting what’s next,” said Austin Rosen, Founder & CEO of Electric Feel Entertainment.\nTuesday, August 18\nBritish rock band Wolf Alice has signed with UTA for touring representation in North America, the company announced on Tuesday.\nThe signing comes as the quartet is slated to support Olivia Rodrigo on the first leg of her “The Unraveled Tour.” Kicking off on September 25, Wolf Alice will take to arenas across the U.S. over 18 sold-out dates. Earlier this month, the band performed at the Lollapalooza festival and played a sold-out show at New York City’s Pier 17. The band’s fourth and most recent studio album “The Clearing” was produced by Greg Kurstin (Adele, Paul McCartney, Pink, Foo Fighters), who won the Grammy producer of the year award two consecutive years.\nMonday, August 17\nRCS Worldwide has announced the promotion of Alissa Pollack to president of Mediabase, a top music industry source for airplay analytics, chart intelligence, and music data. In this elevated role, Pollack will also continue her full responsibilities as president of global music marketing at iHeartMedia.\nAs president, Pollack will lead Mediabase’s strategic direction, global expansion, product innovation, and commercial growth, according to the announcement.\nSusan Larkin, RCS Worldwide president-CEO, said, “Alissa has spent her career at the intersection of music, data, and relationships, which is exactly where Mediabase lives. This promotion is a recognition of everything she has already built and a reflection of our confidence in where she will take Mediabase next. As she steps into this role she will have my full support as she leads Mediabase forward.”\nPollack said, “I’m honored to lead Mediabase at such an exciting moment in its evolution while continuing the work I love at iHeartMedia. These roles are incredibly complementary because they share the same purpose: helping the industry make smarter decisions through meaningful insights.\n“As one of my first initiatives, I’m thrilled to make more Mediabase chart data publicly accessible through our newly updated Mediabase.com site. Music fans, industry professionals, and artists can now view the Top 50 charts across every format in both the U.S. and Canada, providing greater visibility into music performance and trends across North America.”\n+ Valerie Pensa, a multiple Clio Award winner who most recently was head of marketing at RCA Records, has left the company to launch of new venture Moonstruck Management & Marketing Inc., which is based in Los Angeles. Over the years she has worked closely with such artists as Tate McRae, P!nk, Doja Cat, Miley Cyrus, Sia, Lisa and more.\nPensa said, “Moonstruck means dreamily entranced — devoted to something you believe in. That’s exactly how I feel about artists with real vision, and it’s the only way I know how to work. This industry doesn’t sit still and my job is to stay dedicated to that vision no matter how the business evolves.”\n+ Jet Management, the Venice, CA-based music management focused on dance music, has announced a new joint management partnership with Debonair (DBNR), the Belgium-based boutique music management company, launching with the acclaimed French electronic music duo Polo & Pan, who are currently on a North American tour as the support act for Jet Management act Empire of the Sun. DBNR’s roster also includes Charlotte de Witte, Enrico Sangiuliano, Bazart and Oliver Symons; Jet’s also manages Justice, Foster the PeopleSam Gellaitry, Neil Frances, WhoMadeWho and others; it is also a partner of the company Firebird.\n“I’ve been a longtime fan of Polo & Pan and genuinely enjoyed their records and live shows long before this collaboration came together,” said DBNR founder-CEO Alexander Vandriessche. “We’ve always had a lot of respect for what Jet built and how they approach artists globally, and with Polo & Pan and this new partnership, there was a very organic fit between both teams and it felt like the right project to start building something together.”\nJet co-founder Tyler Goldberg added, “We are thrilled for the opportunity to both work with one of the most creative and talented artists in the world, one which we’ve admired for years, and to partner with one of the best managers in our industry, our dear friend Alexander Vandriessche.”\n+ Mom+Pop Music has signed singer-songwriter Crash the Party. Signed by label co-founder Michael Goldstone and Katie Fagan, the artist has released the song “Cold Feet” to launch the partnership.\nCrash the Party says, “Growing up in rural Georgia, pursuing music sometimes felt a little delusional. I knew I wasn’t meant to work in a factory, lay cable, or follow the paths a lot of my neighbors took — I just knew I had to make music. Being with Mom+Pop is truly a dream, and it feels like validation that I’m on the right path, crazy or not!” More music will be released later in the fall.\n+ Fenix Entertainment has announced a global partnership with Maluma to lead the development and strategy behind his upcoming world tour, taking place in 2027 and 2028.The 35-year-old company currently works with Luis Miguel, Ricardo Arjona, and Ricardo Montaner, while also developing strategic partnerships with Shakira across multiple markets.\nMarcelo Fígoli, founder and president of Fenix Entertainment, said: “Working with an artist with Maluma’s career and international reach is both a challenge and an enormous responsibility. We believe in his vision, his talent, and the exciting moment he is experiencing in his career. We are incredibly proud to be part of this new chapter and to put our team and experience behind a project of this scale.”\n+ Warner Records Nashville has reshaped its marketing team structure with the announcement of several staff promotions and newly created roles:\nAddison Nunes is being promoted to VP of marketing where she will oversee marketing duties for one half of the roster alongside Anna Pittman, who will lead marketing efforts for one half as well, continuing her role as VP of marketing.\nJustin Luffman will move into the newly created role of VP of culture & creative marketing. In this role, he will act as a driving force behind our artists’ identities and cultural relevance, according to the announcement.\nPittman will be supported by Jackson Ammons, who moves into his new role as marketing manager. Nunes is supported by new hire Jennipher Miller, who begins her new role as marketing manager, while Samantha Nepsa assumes Nunes’ previous duties with her promotion to senior analyst of data & analytics.\nAllyson Kim will move into the role of manager of touring & fan activations, while Alyssa Cohen joins the marketing team as coordinator, reporting to EVP of marketing Victoria Mason.\n+ United Talent Agency announced today the addition of six new executive appointments, along with 11 internal promotions across the music division. The announcement is the latest in a series of advancements for the department, including the recent elevation of James Wright to co-head of U.K. music, the announcement of seven new music partners in March, and the promotion of 19 colleagues in December.\nChris Anderson (Agent), Randy Freedman (Agent, Music Brand Partnerships), Jordan Golenberg (Agent), and Lauren Katzakian (Executive, Business Affairs) are based out of Los Angeles. Anderson joins UTA following nearly two decades in artist management and talent representation. With over a decade of experience at Universal Music Group and elsewhere, Katzakian is the newest member of the UTA Business Affairs team focusing on touring and Music Brand Partnerships. Freedman also arrives via UMG, and will focus his efforts within the Music Brand Partnerships division. Golenberg joins from Day After Day Productions role.\nAnthony Brown (Agent) and Becky Drouet (Agent) join the ranks of agents in London. Brown brings over 13 years of industry experience from CAA, where he specialized in hip-hop and R&B. Drouet will work across touring and corporate/private group, following more than 18 years at LPO.\nUTA’s newest coordinator class will support agents and executives across several divisions within the music department, including touring, marketing, brand partnerships, music crossover, A&R and more. Juan Aguirre, Lindsey Cole, Alia Lopotanak and Mark Soleymani are the newest coordinators in the Los Angeles office; Julian Huff, Pearse Matteson, and Jenna Shatzman have been upped to coordinators in New York; Cat Ingersoll is the latest to be elevated to coordinator in Nashville; and Dylan Bryant, Brooke Newton and Emily Rhodes are the latest coordinator elevations in London.",
        "content": "Peermusic has signed a worldwide publishing deal with My Morning Jacket and frontman Jim James, as announced by Jamie Cerreta, President of U.S. and Canada. Through the deal, Peermusic will publish the go-forward works of My Morning Jacket as well as Jim James’ go-forward solo recordings, starting with his new album “Wowed Out,” due out August 28 on ATO Records. The deal marks a decades-long publi...",
