        "image": "https://images.ctfassets.net/lzny33ho1g45/29wnIA3sZnRJpmjOB1HAmb/2aeec6fe1865d91c4c152c0ad4179d30/best-salesforce-alternatives-hero.jpg",
        "full_content": "We independently review every app we recommend in our best apps lists. When you click some of the links on this page, we may earn a commission. Learn more.\nOnce you're in the Salesforce universe, it's hard to move out. Part of that is because it's getting the job done: it can do almost anything, it scales to almost any size, and its ecosystem is enormous. If you have a dedicated Salesforce admin, a development team, and a budget, it's the gold standard for CRM, by a long shot.\nBut if you're ready to move on—and lots of people are—there are plenty of Salesforce alternatives that cover a lot of the same ground Salesforce does, at a fraction of the cost. Others take a completely different approach and turn out to be a better fit for teams that don't need the Death Star to manage their pipeline.\nBased on extensive research and testing by app testers at Zapier, here are the Salesforce alternatives worth switching to.\nThe best Salesforce alternatives\n- HubSpot for a full-featured Salesforce alternative\n- Pipedrive for sales simplicity\n- Zoho CRM for an affordable alternative\n- Vtiger for small businesses\n- Attio for an AI-native CRM\n- Nutshell for managing a sales team\n- Copper for Google Workspace users\nWhat makes the best Salesforce alternative?\nHow we evaluate and test apps\nOur best apps roundups are written by humans who've spent much of their careers using, testing, and writing about software. Unless explicitly stated, we spend dozens of hours researching and testing apps, using each app as it's intended to be used and evaluating it against the criteria we set for the category. We're never paid for placement in our articles from any app or for links to any site—we value the trust readers put in us to offer authentic evaluations of the categories and apps we review. For more details on our process, read the full rundown of how we select apps to feature on the Zapier blog.\nThe bar for a Salesforce alternative is whether it can actually replace the things Salesforce does well—including pipeline management, reporting, automation, and integrations—without requiring a full-time administrator to keep it running. Here's what I was looking for as I reviewed all the testing notes and put this list together:\n- Full CRM fundamentals. This is things like contact and deal tracking, a visual pipeline, and activity logging. It doesn't have to be as ridiculously robust as Salesforce, but it needs to cover the essentials.\n- Automation. The whole point of a CRM is to take work off your teams' plates. If you're doing manual work inside of it, you're just adding that work back in, so all the CRMs on this list have nice automation features.\n- Reporting. Salesforce reports are powerful but not always legible to normal humans. The best alternatives make insights accessible without a business intelligence team.\n- Easy adoption. Salesforce's notorious learning curve is a big reason lots of teams want to switch, so I prioritized tools that are easy to learn, without sacrificing depth of features.\n- Value. Another common reason teams leave Salesforce is the slow creep of per-user costs and add-ons. I looked for alternatives where the pricing model doesn't turn on you at scale.\nSalesforce alternatives at a glance\n|  | Best for | Standout features | Pricing | \n|---|---|---|---|\n| HubSpot | A full-featured Salesforce alternative | Every GTM feature you could possibly need | Free plan available; paid plans from $7/user/month | \n| Pipedrive | Sales simplicity | Visual pipeline designed by salespeople | From $14/user/month | \n| Zoho CRM | An affordable alternative | AI with predictive analytics and anomaly detection | Free plan available; paid plans from $14/user/month | \n| Vtiger | Small businesses | Sales, help desk, marketing, and projects in one platform | From $12/user/month | \n| Attio | An AI-native CRM | AI at the core of all the features | Free plan available; paid plans from $35/user/month | \n| Nutshell | Managing a sales team | Territory-based assignment | From $13/user/month | \n| Copper | Google Workspace users | Native Gmail/Drive/Calendar integration | From $23/user/month | \nBest Salesforce alternative for a full-featured replacement\nHubSpot\nHubSpot pros:\n- Covers most of the ground Salesforce does\n- Two-way data sync with other apps via Data Hub\n- Lots of native integrations\nHubSpot cons:\n- Free plan is much more limited than in the past\n- Pricing gets complicated (and expensive) as you scale up\nHubSpot is the most direct replacement for Salesforce on this list by far. It covers sales, marketing, customer service, content management, operations, and commerce—a scope that rivals Salesforce's at a fraction of the entry price. On the free plan alone, you get a visual pipeline, basic reporting, email tracking, a meeting scheduler, live chat, and access to thousands of native integrations. Salesforce doesn't have a free tier that competes with any of that.\nUnlike Salesforce, though, HubSpot manages to pack a comparable feature set into an interface that a new rep can actually navigate on their first day. Even though it's a sprawling product, it's pretty cohesive: whether you're looking at a contact record, a marketing email, or a support ticket, the experience feels like the same product.\nHubSpot Breeze, the built-in AI, is baked into the workflows you're already doing rather than bolted on as a separate module. Breeze Assistant can run actions on your CRM—doing things like creating and updating records, drafting outreach, and pulling contact enrichment—from a side-tab chat interface available across all the Hubs. There's also the Agent Hub, which has purpose-built agents for things like prospecting and data management.\nOnce you start needing enterprise features, HubSpot's pricing can spiral in a Salesforce-like way. The per-Hub upgrade model means you can end up paying a lot to unlock things that feel like they should be included. If you're leaving Salesforce because of cost, don't upgrade all the way to Enterprise HubSpot in year two and expect a different feeling.\nHubSpot also integrates with Zapier, which means you can connect it to thousands of other tools and build AI-powered end-to-end workflows. For example, whenever a new contact is created in HubSpot, you can automatically enroll them in the right email sequence and post a notification to your sales team in Slack—all without manual intervention. You can also use Zapier to access and take action on HubSpot data without ever leaving your AI chat window. Learn more about how to automate HubSpot.\nHubSpot pricing: Free plan available; paid plans from $7/user/month (billed annually)\nRead more: HubSpot vs. Salesforce\nBest Salesforce alternative for sales\nPipedrive\nPipedrive pros:\n- Visual pipeline designed by salespeople\n- AI features available on the lowest-tier plan\n- Easy workflow automation with clear documentation\nPipedrive cons:\n- Can get expensive quickly with add-ons\n- Not built for multi-department complexity\nPipedrive takes a different approach than Salesforce. Instead of doing everything, it does sales extremely well and gets out of the way. The pipeline view is the whole point: deal cards with dynamic status icons, color-coded urgency signals, one-click call logging, and other touches that salespeople expect and often don't find in multi-purpose tools.\nPipedrive was designed by salespeople who were frustrated with the CRMs they had to use, and that origin story is visible throughout the product. Nothing feels cluttered. When you're coming off a call, the app prompts you to log the outcome immediately, attaches the recording, and moves you to the next one. The contact timeline helps you space out touchpoints so you're not drowning a lead in emails. Duplicate detection catches that new contact you just added from the trade show before you create a mess.\nThe AI Sales Assistant identifies high-potential deals, surfaces at-risk ones, and suggests next actions based on your actual patterns. There's also the standard email generation, summarization, and AI-generated reports, and none of it requires configuration beyond what you'd do when setting up a normal deal.\nPipedrive wasn't built for teams that need marketing automation, customer support, and sales in the same system, but if your use case is primarily the pipeline, it's definitely the app you want. (If you need more, you'll be paying for add-ons or patching in other tools.)\nPipedrive integrates with Zapier, so you can access and take action on your CRM data automatically or directly from your AI chat window. For example, whenever a deal reaches a key stage in Pipedrive, you can trigger an AI workflow to generate a personalized follow-up email, log it in Google Sheets, and notify the right team member in Slack—automatically. Learn more about how to automate Pipedrive.\nPipedrive pricing: From $14/user/month (billed annually); add-ons priced separately\nRead more: Pipedrive vs. HubSpot\nBest affordable Salesforce alternative\nZoho CRM\nZoho CRM pros:\n- Enterprise-grade feature set at accessible pricing\n- AI for predictive analytics, anomaly detection, and best-time-to-contact\n- Free plan available; stays affordable through the higher tiers\nZoho CRM cons:\n- Interface can feel cluttered\n- Steeper learning curve than some competitors\n- A little buggy\nZoho CRM is the budget option here. Much like when I bullied my landscaper brother into adding my lawn into his weekly route for free, Zoho CRM unlocks a ton of benefits for way less money.\nZia AI does data enrichment using email data, predictive conversion likelihood for leads and deals, next-best-action suggestions, best-time-to-contact recommendations, and historical-based forecasting. It detects anomalies in your sales data—unusual drops in closed deals, weird pipeline behavior—and flags them before they become problems. It runs sentiment and intent analysis on emails and suggests workflow automations and macros based on what it observes in your actual patterns. In our testing, we did encounter some bugs, especially with the AI agents, but if you're willing to troubleshoot a bit, you're getting AI CRM at a fraction of the cost.\nBeyond AI, the core CRM is robust: multichannel marketing, help desk, social integration, and a massive ecosystem of other Zoho products that integrate seamlessly. The user interface isn't the prettiest, but you can't really complain when it means you don't have to beg your finance team for access.\nZoho CRM integrates with Zapier, connecting it to thousands of other apps to maximize your team's time. For example, you can automatically pull in new Google Ads leads as Zoho records and trigger personalized email sequences based on their behavior—without any manual data entry. You can also use Zapier MCP to access your CRM and take action in it straight from whatever AI chatbot you use. Learn more about how to automate Zoho CRM.\nZoho CRM pricing: Free plan available for up to 3 users; paid plans from $14/user/month (billed annually)\nRead more: Salesforce vs. Zoho CRM\nBest Salesforce alternative for small businesses\nVtiger\nVtiger pros:\n- Sales, help desk, marketing, and project management in one platform\n- Each module is highly customizable\n- Two-tier user pricing based on module access needs\nVtiger cons:\n- Interface feels dated in places\n- Learning curve is steeper than lighter CRMs\nA lot of small businesses are paying enterprise prices for features they'll never use on Salesforce—and it's all administered by someone who learned Salesforce in a previous life and now spends a meaningful chunk of their week just keeping it running. Vtiger is the antidote to that specific situation.\nVtiger handles sales, marketing, help desk, inventory, project management, and documents in a single platform that doesn't cost enterprise money. And the modules are deeply connected: close a deal and convert it to a project in one click, link your most-requested products to a contact's record, or track open support cases from the same screen as your pipeline. For a small team wearing multiple hats, the consolidation alone is worth it.\nThe user interface takes some getting used to. Vtiger has been around for a while, and that history shows in places. But the navigation is logical: a top menu keeps each module accessible, and a hamburger icon on the left opens a full-screen jump menu. Once you know where everything lives, the day-to-day experience moves quickly.\nCalculus AI is Vtiger's intelligence layer. It scores deals based on historical patterns, suggests next best actions, and generates reply recommendations for emails and support cases. For teams coming off Salesforce's Agentforce, it won't have the same depth of customization—for example, you can't build custom predictive models from scratch unless you're on a higher tier. But for most small businesses, the out-of-the-box predictive features are more than sufficient, and they actually work without a data science background to configure them.\nVtiger integrates with Zapier, which means you can build AI-orchestrated workflows across your entire tech stack. For example, whenever a new lead is created in Vtiger, you can automatically add them to a Mailchimp email sequence and update the deal stage based on their engagement behavior. Learn more about how to automate Vtiger.\nVtiger pricing: From $12/user/month (billed annually)\nBest AI-native Salesforce alternative\nAttio\nAttio pros:\n- Highly customizable data model\n- AI attributes that auto-populate fields based on CRM data\n- Email sequences with dynamic AI-generated content per contact\n- Free plan includes AI features and unlimited seats\nAttio cons:\n- Lightweight on advanced CRM features like forecasting and territory management\n- Relatively new, so the ecosystem is still maturing\nMost CRMs added AI after the fact. And while Salesforce has done a great job integrating AI into a decades-old technology, Attio is fully AI-native.\nThe core of what makes Attio different is the AI attributes feature. Instead of fields that humans fill in, AI attributes are fields that think for themselves. You configure them with instructions (e.g., summarize this deal, classify this contact's intent, extract key themes from this note), and the attribute generates and updates the output automatically as the underlying data changes. You can apply the same AI attributes to people, companies, deals, or any custom object you create. It's a different relationship between your data and your CRM than Salesforce's record-and-report model.\nThe email sequences take a similar approach: you write a sequence with time delays and conditions, like any drip campaign, but inside each message, you can add AI content variables. Write a prompt for what you want in a specific section (something like \"explain how our product addresses the challenge they mentioned in their last call\"), and the AI generates that section fresh for each recipient, grounded in live CRM data. The result is personalization at scale that doesn't require a team to manually customize every email.\nThe underlying CRM is equally as flexible. Objects, pipelines, and lists aren't fixed: you define them based on your actual go-to-market motion. Salesforce offers this kind of flexibility too, but Attio's version doesn't require a Salesforce admin to set up.\nKeep in mind, though, that Attio is a newer product, so it's not as robust and not as battle-tested. There's no forecasting, no territory management, and no deep sales team reporting. But if you're a growth-stage company that wants a modern, AI-native data layer for your GTM motion, Attio is one of the most interesting options available.\nAttio also integrates with Zapier, so you can connect it to your entire tech stack and take action on your CRM data straight from whatever AI chat tool your sales team relies on.\nAttio pricing: Free plan available for 3 seats, 50,000 records, and AI attributes; paid plans from $35/user/month (billed annually)\nBest Salesforce alternative for managing a sales team\nNutshell\nNutshell pros:\n- Territory-based lead assignment with granular geographic rules\n- Deep reporting with forecasting and team-level breakdowns\n- Marketing, sales, and engagement features in a single subscription\nNutshell cons:\n- Some add-ons can stack up in cost\n- Occasional glitches\nNutshell is the CRM that most obviously thinks about what a sales manager actually needs to do their job. The features that set it apart—things like territory-based assignment, granular reporting, and team-level forecasting—are exactly the things that make Salesforce feel worth its price until you realize you're paying thousands of dollars a month for features you'd get here for $13/user.\nTerritory-based management lets you set geographic rules—down to the postal code level—that automatically route incoming leads to the right rep or team. It's the kind of feature that usually shows up in enterprise CRMs at enterprise prices, but Nutshell builds it in. Reporting is also deep: you'll get stock reports for all the main pipeline metrics, plus breakdowns by territory, team member, and source. And forecasting lets you project how current performance may play out.\nFor managers, the notification controls mean you can configure exactly when to be alerted—for example, a deal stuck in a stage or a rep who hasn't logged activity in days—and choose whether to receive those alerts in-app or by email.\nAI in Nutshell is focused on compression and enrichment. It summarizes timelines for people, companies, and leads so you can get caught up on a relationship in a paragraph instead of scrolling through 40 activity entries.\nNutshell also integrates with Zapier, which means you can connect it to the tools your team already uses, build automated workflows, and act on your CRM data straight from your AI assistant.\nNutshell pricing: From $13/user/month (billed annually) for unlimited contacts and storage.\nBest Salesforce alternative for Google Workspace users\nCopper\nCopper pros:\n- Native Gmail/Drive/Calendar integration (CRM lives inside your inbox)\n- Automatically imports contacts from existing email threads\n- Lead scoring, predictive sales insights, and email sentiment analysis\nCopper cons:\n- No integration with the Microsoft ecosystem\n- AI features are strategic and insights-based, not generative\nIf your sales team spends most of their day in Gmail and Google Calendar, how much time are they spending switching over to your CRM to log what just happened? If the answer is anything other than \"none,\" Copper might be a good fit for your team.\nCopper is a Google Partner, and that relationship is the whole value proposition. The CRM lives inside Gmail as a side tab, inside Drive, and inside Calendar. When you connect your account for the first time, it imports your existing email conversations and adds people as contacts automatically. When you're looking at an email thread, Copper surfaces all the related Docs, Sheets, and Slides in a tab on the side. You never have to leave your inbox to do CRM work.\nThe web dashboard exists for when you need the full-screen experience, but the design philosophy is about reducing tab switching. The experience feels like Google Workspace got a CRM layer instead of a CRM app that sort of works with Google Workspace.\nAI in Copper focuses on the strategic stuff: lead scoring based on communication history and behavioral patterns, predictive sales insights, and email sentiment analysis. It won't write your emails for you (Google Gemini is already doing that in Gmail, and two AI assistants fighting to draft your replies would be chaotic), but it will tell you which leads are heating up, which deals are at risk, and where your pipeline is most likely to close.\nCopper integrates with Zapier, extending it to thousands of apps beyond the Google ecosystem. For example, you can automatically create Copper leads from new form submissions, sync deal updates to a database for reporting, and trigger Slack notifications when high-value leads change stage. Learn more about how to automate Copper.\nCopper pricing: Plans start at $23/user/month (billed annually)\nWhich Salesforce alternative should you choose?\nIf you need a genuine like-for-like Salesforce replacement, HubSpot is the obvious answer. If your team is primarily focused on the pipeline, go with Pipedrive. If neither of those is the right fit, scan the rest of the list to find the app that solves your niche problem—one of these apps will do the trick. And if you want to drown in even more options, take a look at some other lists of the best CRMs:\n- The best CRM software\n- The best free CRM software\n- The best CRMs for small business\n- The best AI CRM tools\nRelated reading:",
