        "title": "How to Calculate Geyser Power Consumption?",
        "link": "https://paytm.com/blog/bill-payments/electricity-bills/how-to-calculate-geyser-power-consumption/",
        "source": "A Comprehensive Guide to Money Transfer, Recharges, Bill Payments and Other Digital Payments | Paytm Blog",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T21:05:23.142142+00:00",
        "image": "https://wpblogassets.paytm.com/paytmblog/uploads/2021/11/6_electricity_How-to-calculate-geyser-power-consumption.jpg",
        "full_content": "A geyser is one of the heaviest electricity consumers in an Indian home, often adding ₹350 to ₹900 a month to the winter bill. This article shows two ways to work out exactly how many units yours uses, one based on wattage and running time and one based on tank size and water temperature, along with the rupee cost at typical tariffs. It also covers the right capacity for your household, the ideal thermostat setting, what a 5-star rating actually saves, and how to pay your electricity bill on Paytm.\nKnowing what your geyser costs to run is the first step to controlling it. Most households overestimate how long the geyser needs to stay on and underestimate what leaving it on all day costs, and both mistakes show up on the bill.\nThis article covers how to calculate geyser electricity usage two different ways, the factors that push consumption up, practical steps to cut it, and how to pay your electricity bill conveniently with Paytm.\nUnderstanding Geyser Electricity Consumption\nA geyser draws power measured in watts (W) or kilowatts (kW). What you actually pay for is energy, measured in kilowatt hours (kWh), also called units on your electricity bill.\nPower ratings vary by type:\nA higher wattage geyser heats water faster but draws more power while it runs. Because the faster unit finishes sooner, the total units consumed for the same volume of water end up broadly similar. What really drives your bill is how much hot water you use and how long the geyser sits switched on doing nothing.\nMethod 1: Calculate Using Wattage and Running Time\nThe basic formula is:\nEnergy consumed (kWh) = Power rating (kW) × Operating hours (h)\nStep 1: Find the power rating on the geyser’s rating label or manual, for example 2,000 W.\nStep 2: Divide by 1,000 to convert to kilowatts. A 2,000 W geyser is 2 kW.\nStep 3: Estimate the actual heating time per day, not the time the switch is on. A geyser cycles off once the thermostat is satisfied, so a unit left on for four hours may only heat for forty minutes.\nStep 4: Multiply the units by your per unit tariff. Domestic slab rates across Indian states typically range from ₹4 to ₹10 per unit, plus fixed charges. The examples below use ₹8 per unit.\nExample 1: A 2,000 W storage geyser heating for 1 hour a day\nExample 2: A 3,000 W instant geyser heating for 30 minutes a day\nA season is taken as 150 days because most Indian households use a geyser for roughly four to six months, not all year. Calculating on 365 days overstates the annual cost substantially.\nMethod 2: Calculate Using Tank Size and Water Temperature\nMost people cannot estimate heating time accurately, so this method is more reliable. Heating 1 litre of water by 1°C takes about 1.163 watt hours.\nUnits per tank (kWh) = Litres × Temperature rise (°C) × 1.163 ÷ 1,000\nTake a 15 litre geyser in winter, with inlet water at 20°C and the thermostat set to 50°C, so the temperature rise is 30°C:\n15 × 30 × 1.163 = 523 Wh, or roughly 0.52 units per full tank.\nOn a 2,000 W element that takes about 16 minutes, closer to 18 minutes once heat losses are counted.\nUnits and heating time per full tank\nBased on a 30°C temperature rise and a 2,000 W element:\nIn summer the inlet water may already be at 30°C, so the temperature rise drops to around 15°C and consumption roughly halves.\nWhat this means for a typical household\nA family of three using a 15 litre geyser for three heating cycles a day consumes about 1.6 units daily. That works out to roughly ₹12.50 a day, ₹375 a month, and about ₹1,900 across a five month winter at ₹8 per unit. At a ₹4 tariff the same usage costs around ₹190 a month, and at ₹10 it costs around ₹470.\nHow Much Hot Water Do You Actually Need?\nConsumption follows usage, so it helps to know the volumes involved. A bucket bath uses 15 to 20 litres in total, of which 8 to 12 litres is hot water at 50°C mixed with cold. A shower delivers 6 to 10 litres a minute, so an eight minute shower uses 50 to 80 litres in total. This is why shower users need a larger tank and see noticeably higher bills than bucket bath users.\nChoosing the right capacity\nAn oversized geyser heats more water than you draw, and the surplus simply cools down and gets reheated, which is pure waste.\nKey Factors Influencing Geyser Electricity Use\nVolume of hot water used: the single biggest factor. Every extra litre drawn is a litre the geyser must heat again.\nTemperature setting: most Indian thermostats adjust between 25°C and 75°C and leave the factory set around 55°C to 60°C. Every 10°C reduction cuts the energy needed for that tank by roughly a third of the temperature rise.\nStandby time: a storage geyser left switched on continuously loses heat through the tank wall and reheats repeatedly, even if nobody uses hot water.\nInlet water temperature: in winter, incoming water in north India can drop to 12°C to 18°C, against 28°C to 32°C in summer. The same bath can cost double in January.\nInsulation quality: thicker PUF insulation holds heat longer. Older tanks with degraded insulation reheat far more often.\nScale buildup: in hard water areas, limescale coats the heating element and forces it to run longer for the same result.\nGeyser capacity: a 25 litre tank needs roughly 1.7 times the energy of a 15 litre tank to heat fully.\nWhat a BEE Star Rating Actually Saves\nFor storage water heaters, the BEE label rates standing loss, meaning the units lost per 24 hours when the geyser is kept on but unused. A 5-star 15 litre model typically loses around 0.5 to 0.7 kWh per 24 hours, while a 1-star model of the same size loses closer to 1.0 to 1.3 kWh. Exact figures are printed on the label and vary by model.\nIf you leave the geyser switched on all day, that gap is roughly 0.5 units a day, about ₹4 daily, ₹120 a month, or ₹600 across a winter season. Over a ten year life the star rating easily repays the ₹1,000 to ₹2,500 price premium. If you only switch the geyser on when needed, the saving is much smaller, because standing loss barely applies.\nNote that BEE star labelling covers storage type electric water heaters. Instant geysers are generally not star rated.\nPractical Tips to Reduce Geyser Electricity Bills\n- Switch on only for the time needed: use the heating times in the table above as a guide. A 15 litre tank rarely needs more than 20 minutes.\n- Set the thermostat to 50°C to 55°C: this is comfortable for bathing and reduces scale formation. Settings above 60°C raise both consumption and scalding risk, since water at 60°C can scald in about five seconds.\n- Do not leave it on overnight: standing loss on a poorly rated tank can add 1 unit or more a day for water nobody uses.\n- Fit a timer: a geyser timer costs ₹500 to ₹2,000 and switches the unit on 20 minutes before you need hot water. It usually pays for itself within one season.\n- Descale annually: professional servicing costs ₹400 to ₹900 and restores heating efficiency lost to limescale. A heavily scaled element can take 20 to 30 percent longer to heat the same tank.\n- Add an insulation jacket: for older tanks, a jacket costs ₹600 to ₹1,500 and reduces standby heat loss.\n- Insulate the hot water pipe: worthwhile if the geyser is more than 3 to 4 metres from the outlet, since the first litre or two otherwise runs cold and gets wasted.\n- Prefer bucket baths: a bucket bath uses 8 to 12 litres of hot water against 30 to 40 litres for a typical shower.\n- Buy a 5-star storage model: expect to pay ₹4,000 to ₹8,000 for a 6 litre unit, ₹6,000 to ₹13,000 for 10 to 15 litres, and ₹9,000 to ₹18,000 for 25 litres.\n- Check the electrical setup: a 2,000 W geyser needs a 16 A socket and dedicated wiring, and a 4,500 W instant geyser typically needs a dedicated 20 A to 25 A circuit. Undersized wiring is both a safety hazard and a source of losses.\nPaying Your Electricity Bill Conveniently with Paytm\nManaging electricity bills is simple and secure with Paytm. You can pay anytime, set reminders so you never miss a due date, and keep a record of every payment in one place.\nHere is how to pay your electricity bill using the Paytm app:\nStep 1: Open the Paytm app on your smartphone.\nStep 2: Tap “Recharge & Pay Bills” or the “Electricity” icon on the home screen.\nStep 3: Select your state and then your electricity board, for example BSES Rajdhani Power Limited.\nStep 4: Enter your consumer number or account ID, which appears on any previous bill.\nStep 5: The app fetches your bill details. Verify the amount and the consumer name.\nStep 6: Pay using your preferred method, such as UPI, debit card, credit card\nStep 7: Complete the payment and receive instant confirmation in the app and by SMS or email.\nYou can save biller details for faster payments later, and view every past payment under “Balance & History” in the app, which makes it easy to compare winter and summer bills and see exactly what the geyser added.",
        "content": "A geyser is one of the heaviest electricity consumers in an Indian home, often adding ₹350 to ₹900 a month to the winter bill. This article shows two ways to work out exactly how many units yours uses, one based on wattage and running time and one based on tank size and water temperature, along with the rupee cost at typical tariffs. It also covers the right capacity for your household, the ideal ...",
