        "title": "5 Money Mistakes to Avoid When You Start Earning | Paytm Postpaid",
        "link": "https://paytm.com/blog/loan/postpaid-loan/5-money-mistakes-to-avoid-when-you-start-earning-paytm-postpaid/",
        "source": "A Comprehensive Guide to Money Transfer, Recharges, Bill Payments and Other Digital Payments | Paytm Blog",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T21:05:23.142127+00:00",
        "image": "https://wpblogassets.paytm.com/paytmblog/uploads/2026/08/Postpaid-3.png",
        "full_content": "Getting your first salary feels like a milestone. For the first time, you have money that you’ve earned yourself and the freedom to decide how you want to spend it.\nBut earning your first salary also comes with a few new responsibilities. Without a plan, it’s easy to fall into spending habits that can make managing your money harder later.\nHere are five common money mistakes to avoid when you start earning and how you can build healthier money habits from the beginning.\n1. Letting lifestyle inflation take over\nA higher income can quickly lead to higher expenses.\nYou get your first salary and suddenly that weekend dinner, new phone, extra subscriptions or frequent cabs start feeling more affordable. The problem is when your lifestyle grows as quickly as your income, leaving little room for savings or unexpected expenses.\nYou don’t have to stop yourself from enjoying your salary. Instead, set a limit for discretionary spending and make sure your savings and essential expenses are taken care of first.\nThe goal is to let your income grow faster than your lifestyle.\n2. Waiting to start saving and investing\nOne common thought when you start earning is, “I’ll start saving when I earn more.”\nBut waiting for the perfect salary to start saving can mean missing the opportunity to build a consistent habit early. You don’t necessarily need to start with a large amount. Even setting aside a small portion of your income regularly can help you get into the habit of saving.\nAs your income grows, you can gradually increase the amount you save and invest.\nStart small, stay consistent and give your money time to grow.\n3. Treating credit like extra income\nHaving access to credit can make it easier to manage a short-term expense, but it’s important to remember that credit isn’t additional income.\nBefore using a credit facility, think about when and how you’ll repay it. This is especially important when you’re just starting your earning journey and learning to manage your monthly cash flow.\nWith Paytm Postpaid, you can get a credit limit of up to ₹60,000 and use Credit on Paytm UPI for payments at millions of stores, online platforms and within the Paytm app wherever supported. You can get up to 45 days of credit, depending on when you make the payment.\nThe key is to use credit as a tool for short-term flexibility, not as a reason to spend beyond what you can comfortably repay.\n4. Not planning for unexpected expenses\nUnexpected expenses don’t wait for payday.\nA sudden purchase, an urgent booking or an unplanned bill can throw off your monthly budget if you haven’t left any room for it. That’s why building an emergency fund should be part of your money plan from the beginning.\nAt the same time, having access to short-term credit can provide additional flexibility when an unexpected expense comes up.\nPaytm Postpaid can help you manage a short-term spending need with access to Credit on Paytm UPI, while you plan for repayment later.\nJust remember: credit comes with a repayment obligation, so use it thoughtfully and keep your repayment in mind.\n5. Not knowing where your salary is going\nYou don’t need to track every rupee forever, but when you’re starting out, knowing where your money goes can make a big difference.\nYour salary may look substantial when it arrives, but smaller expenses can quickly add up. Food, travel, shopping, subscriptions and entertainment can all take a larger share of your income than you realise.\nTry reviewing your spending at the end of each month. Look at what you spent on essentials, what went towards savings and where you spent more than planned.\nYou can’t manage your money better until you know where it’s going.\nBuild better money habits from your first salary\nStarting your earning journey isn’t about getting every financial decision right from day one. It’s about building habits that can grow with you.\nKeep lifestyle inflation in check. Start saving early. Know the difference between income and credit. Prepare for unexpected expenses. And keep an eye on where your money goes.\nWhen you need short-term spending flexibility, Paytm Postpaid gives you access to Credit on Paytm UPI with a credit limit of up to ₹60,000 and up to 45 days of credit, depending on when you make the payment. Find Paytm Postpaid in the Paytm app today.",
